and we ran the model and it got loss within 0.05.

12
00:00:42,980 --> 00:00:44,580
So I thought that was pretty cool.

13
00:00:44,580 --> 00:00:48,980
And if you extrapolate out to GPT-5 level performance,

14
00:00:48,980 --> 00:00:50,220
this is the loss you get.

15
00:00:50,220 --> 00:00:53,880
Of course, your mileage might vary depending

16
00:00:53,880 --> 00:00:56,840
on how these scaling loss are.

17
00:00:56,840 --> 00:01:00,880
OK, I just wanted to share that news.

18
00:01:00,880 --> 00:01:05,560
So last lecture I gave an overview of the entire class.

19
00:01:05,560 --> 00:01:07,800
And we are talking about tokenization,

20
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:10,200
which is going to be on the first assignment.

21
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:15,680
Today, I want to talk about resource accounting, which

22
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:20,400
is going to be more on the systems side of things.

23
00:01:20,400 --> 00:01:26,160
So to recall, the main thing we're trying to do

24
00:01:26,160 --> 00:01:31,460
is train the best model we can given a finite set of resources,

25
00:01:31,460 --> 00:01:34,580
which could be compute, memory, sometimes data.

26
00:01:34,580 --> 00:01:37,600
But that's not really going to be a limiting factor for us

27
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:42,720
in this class. And our goal is to simply maximize

28
00:01:42,720 --> 00:01:46,440
the computational efficiency of our training.

29
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:49,780
So before you can optimize the computational efficiency,

30
00:01:49,780 --> 00:01:53,880
we need to understand the efficiency of a given computation.

31
00:01:53,880 --> 00:01:56,040
And for that, we need to understand the compute

32
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,470
and memory characteristics.

33
00:01:58,470 --> 00:02:01,270
Just to give you a taste of the type of questions

34
00:02:01,270 --> 00:02:04,875
you will hopefully be able to answer by the end of the class,

35
00:02:04,875 --> 00:02:05,750
so here's a question.

36
00:02:05,750 --> 00:02:08,070
How long would it take to train a 70 billion

37
00:02:08,070 --> 00:02:12,750
parameter model on 15 trillion tokens on 1024--

38
00:02:12,750 --> 00:02:17,530
actually, this should be H100. So how do you answer that?

39
00:02:17,530 --> 00:02:19,310
Well, there's a formula, which we'll

40
00:02:19,310 --> 00:02:22,030
talk about how you can get the number of FLOPs

41
00:02:22,030 --> 00:02:24,390
to be 6 times the number of parameters

42
00:02:24,390 --> 00:02:26,990
times the number of tokens.

43
00:02:26,990 --> 00:02:32,030
We can look up the spec sheet to see how fast the H100 is.

44
00:02:32,030 --> 00:02:36,550
We have this thing called MFU, which we'll talk about 0.5.

45
00:02:36,550 --> 00:02:40,350
Then you can estimate the number of FLOPs

46
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:44,670
that you need that the hardware gives you per day.

47
00:02:44,670 --> 00:02:46,730
And then you can compute the number of days.

48
00:02:46,730 --> 00:02:51,410
So the number of days is 143. Here's another question.

49
00:02:51,410 --> 00:02:56,790
What's the largest model you can train on H100s using AdamW?

50
00:02:59,390 --> 00:03:08,310
So you can look at, well, H100s have 80 gigabytes of each VM

51
00:03:08,310 --> 00:03:15,030
memory, the number of bytes per parameter, which is 2 plus 2

52
00:03:15,030 --> 00:03:19,510
plus 4 plus 4. We'll explain where that comes from.

53
00:03:19,510 --> 00:03:21,230
And then the number of parameters

54
00:03:21,230 --> 00:03:27,330
is that you can get is going to be about 53 billion.

55
00:03:27,330 --> 00:03:29,230
So there's some caveats here that we

56
00:03:29,230 --> 00:03:31,830
don't count the activations which

57
00:03:31,830 --> 00:03:35,590
depends on batch size and the sequence length.

58
00:03:35,590 --> 00:03:38,870
So this is all very rough back-of-the-envelope calculations.

59
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:40,710
But hopefully by the end of this class

60
00:03:40,710 --> 00:03:43,170
you'll understand where these come from.

61
00:03:43,170 --> 00:03:47,230
And the point is not to precisely calculate

62
00:03:47,230 --> 00:03:50,730
every single thing, but just get the rough shape of things.

63
00:03:54,230 --> 00:03:58,430
OK. So last time I talked about knowledge and what you

64
00:03:58,430 --> 00:04:00,910
can take away from this class--

65
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:03,870
mechanics, which are how things work.

66
00:04:03,870 --> 00:04:05,990
So today, that would be pretty straightforward.

67
00:04:05,990 --> 00:04:09,030
The mechanics are just how PyTorch works, how tensors work.

68
00:04:09,030 --> 00:04:13,910
It should be fairly--there's no magic here.

69
00:04:13,910 --> 00:04:16,670
The mindset I want to impart on you

70
00:04:16,670 --> 00:04:20,910
is that resource accounting is going to be very crucial.

71
00:04:20,910 --> 00:04:22,850
And I want everyone to get in the habit

72
00:04:22,850 --> 00:04:25,910
whenever you write this line of code,

73
00:04:25,910 --> 00:04:30,050
think about the performance characteristics.

74
00:04:30,050 --> 00:04:32,030
And then finally intuitions.

75
00:04:32,030 --> 00:04:36,110
Here we're just going to get a sense of the resources, how

76
00:04:36,110 --> 00:04:39,450
they're spent. There's going to be no ML magic today.

77
00:04:39,450 --> 00:04:43,190
I'll leave that to Tatsu for the next lecture. OK.

78
00:04:43,190 --> 00:04:46,310
So let's get into things.

79
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:49,450
So let's start bottom up and start building up.

80
00:04:49,450 --> 00:04:53,120
So what is at the bottom? At the bottom are tensors.

81
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:56,660
So tensor 是存储一切的基本单元。

82
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:59,720
如果你有参数、梯度、优化器状态，

83
00:04:59,720 --> 00:05:04,380
数据、激活，本质上一切都是 tensor。

84
00:05:04,380 --> 00:05:10,900
比如，你可以看看 DeepSeek 3.2 模型。

85
00:05:10,900 --> 00:05:12,980
你会看到模型本身是

86
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:14,400
一堆不同的 tensor。

87
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,580
每个 tensor 都有一些形状，还有

88
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:20,980
一些精度，我稍后会讲。

89
00:05:20,980 --> 00:05:26,540
所以如你所知，tensor 包含了向量、

90
00:05:26,540 --> 00:05:33,900
矩阵，并且可以推广到任意数量的实体。OK。

91
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:42,420
那我们来谈谈存储 tensor 需要多少开销。

92
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:46,520
这取决于 tensor 的类型。

93
00:05:46,520 --> 00:05:49,590
一般来说，我们会处理那些

94
00:05:49,590 --> 00:05:51,340
存储浮点数的 tensor，但 tensor 也可以

95
00:05:51,340 --> 00:05:56,500
存储整数和其他类型。

96
00:05:56,500 --> 00:06:03,080
对于浮点数，通常当你说到 float，

97
00:06:03,080 --> 00:06:05,940
我认为人们默认指的是 float，

98
00:06:05,940 --> 00:06:08,700
也就是所谓的 float32。

99
00:06:08,700 --> 00:06:14,220
所以一个 float32，如果拆开来看，有 32 位。

100
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:16,300
其中一位是符号位，8 位

101
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,060
是指数，提供了动态范围。

102
00:06:19,060 --> 00:06:23,500
剩下的就是尾数或小数部分，

103
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:26,900
提供了精度变化。

104
00:06:26,900 --> 00:06:34,380
这也称为 fp32 或单精度。

105
00:06:34,380 --> 00:06:37,020
单精度这个词来源于

106
00:06:37,020 --> 00:06:39,960
早期做科学计算的时候，

107
00:06:39,960 --> 00:06:42,040
float32 就像是基准线。

108
00:06:42,040 --> 00:06:44,380
就像如果有人给你一个

109
00:06:44,380 --> 00:06:47,910
float，你会期望它至少是单精度。

110
00:06:47,910 --> 00:06:52,770
如果你想要更高的精度，那么你

111
00:06:52,770 --> 00:06:56,570
可以用双精度，也就是 float64。

112
00:06:56,570 --> 00:06:58,930
但在深度学习中，我们走的是另一个方向，

113
00:06:58,930 --> 00:07:02,250
因为即使 32 位也很多了。

114
00:07:02,250 --> 00:07:05,450
我们想做的那些计算

115
00:07:05,450 --> 00:07:08,130
并不需要像某些

116
00:07:08,130 --> 00:07:11,130
数值模拟那样高的精度。OK。

117
00:07:11,130 --> 00:07:15,250
所以在讲其他类型之前，我们先看看 float32。

118
00:07:15,250 --> 00:07:18,530
那我们构造一个 4 乘 8 的矩阵。

119
00:07:18,530 --> 00:07:23,070
默认情况下，你创建的 tensor 类型是 float32。

120
00:07:23,070 --> 00:07:26,810
所以如果你想要别的类型，你需要声明它。

121
00:07:26,810 --> 00:07:30,010
And the memory usage is just the number of elements times

122
00:07:30,010 --> 00:07:36,330
the element size here, which is 4 bytes for a 32-bit number.

123
00:07:36,330 --> 00:07:38,390
And that's going to be 128 bytes.

124
00:07:41,640 --> 00:07:44,550
So just to give you a kind of perspective,

125
00:07:44,550 --> 00:07:47,150
so in GPT-3, which is a fairly old model

126
00:07:47,150 --> 00:07:50,050
one of the matrix in the feedforward layer

127
00:07:50,050 --> 00:07:59,510
is about 2.3 gigabytes. So these tensors can get quite big.

128
00:07:59,510 --> 00:08:04,390
And this is not even the biggest one that one can imagine.

129
00:08:04,390 --> 00:08:10,170
OK. So since we're interested in efficiency,

130
00:08:10,170 --> 00:08:14,530
we want to generally reduce the amount of storage.

131
00:08:14,530 --> 00:08:19,210
And we'll see that as you reduce the precision,

132
00:08:19,210 --> 00:08:20,870
you actually save memory.

133
00:08:20,870 --> 00:08:26,410
And you also save time because operating on 16 bits

134
00:08:26,410 --> 00:08:30,210
is going to be faster, let's say, twice

135
00:08:30,210 --> 00:08:34,710
as fast, but not always. It depends.

136
00:08:34,710 --> 00:08:38,130
And then memory, by reducing memory,

137
00:08:38,130 --> 00:08:40,049
we'll see later that actually reducing memory

138
00:08:40,049 --> 00:08:43,710
can save time as well, which is maybe less obvious.

139
00:08:43,710 --> 00:08:46,690
But it will hopefully become clear.

140
00:08:46,690 --> 00:08:49,750
So the obvious thing is you say, OK,

141
00:08:49,750 --> 00:08:52,890
let's take away half the bits. Now you have float16.

142
00:08:52,890 --> 00:08:54,910
So float16 says you have a sign.

143
00:08:54,910 --> 00:08:56,530
You have only 5 bits of exponent,

144
00:08:56,530 --> 00:09:00,290
and then the rest is the mantissa.

145
00:09:00,290 --> 00:09:10,810
So float16 is good, except for its dynamic range is poor.

146
00:09:10,810 --> 00:09:15,930
So even if you have, let's say, try to construct 1e minus 8

147
00:09:15,930 --> 00:09:19,010
tensor, then that is actually just 0.

148
00:09:19,010 --> 00:09:20,970
So you can't really represent very big numbers,

149
00:09:20,970 --> 00:09:25,090
and you can't represent very small numbers.

150
00:09:25,090 --> 00:09:27,450
And the reason is that this exponent

151
00:09:27,450 --> 00:09:35,690
it's only 5 bits of exponent compared to 8.

152
00:09:35,690 --> 00:09:44,200
So if you train with fp16, which people did back in the day,

153
00:09:44,200 --> 00:09:47,380
you will get instability. You will get underflow.

154
00:09:47,380 --> 00:09:53,800
You get overflow. You'll get NaNs. It's pretty challenging.

155
00:09:53,800 --> 00:09:58,860
So bfloat16 was invented.

156
00:09:58,860 --> 00:10:03,440
So this was developed in actually 2018 to address this issue.

157
00:10:03,440 --> 00:10:08,320
And the observation was that well, let's not compromise

158
00:10:08,320 --> 00:10:10,600
on the number of bits.

159
00:10:10,600 --> 00:10:13,600
The number of bits is going to be the same as fp16.

160
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:16,520
But we're going to shift some of the bits from the mantissa

161
00:10:16,520 --> 00:10:18,120
to the exponent.

162
00:10:18,120 --> 00:10:22,200
So that means it has more dynamic range than float16.

163
00:10:26,840 --> 00:10:30,240
And it actually has the same dynamic range as float32.

164
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:33,720
But of course, the resolution is worse

165
00:10:33,720 --> 00:10:35,680
because there's no free lunch here.

166
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:38,760
But it turns out that in a lot of deep learning applications,

167
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:40,740
this is well worth the trade-off.

168
00:10:40,740 --> 00:10:44,680
You want the dynamic range to not overflow and underflow.

169
00:10:44,680 --> 00:10:48,280
And because things are kind of sloppy and stochastic anyway,

170
00:10:48,280 --> 00:10:50,620
you don't need that much resolution.

171
00:10:54,800 --> 00:11:01,700
So let me actually skip over this part.So OK.

172
00:11:01,700 --> 00:11:05,800
So to summarize, what are the implications for training?

173
00:11:05,800 --> 00:11:08,920
So you can absolutely train with float32.

174
00:11:08,920 --> 00:11:10,665
And if you're training a small model,

175
00:11:10,665 --> 00:11:13,040
you probably just-- and you don't want to worry about it,

176
00:11:13,040 --> 00:11:21,240
just float32 is fine.But it requires 4 bytes of--

177
00:11:21,240 --> 00:11:25,800
sorry, 4 bytes of memory per float.

178
00:11:25,800 --> 00:11:28,720
And that can take up a lot of memory.

179
00:11:28,720 --> 00:11:34,280
And if you train with float16, then that's

180
00:11:34,280 --> 00:11:42,520
going to be too risky.So bf16 is the sweet spot.

181
00:11:42,520 --> 00:11:45,680
Even bf16 can be risky as well.

182
00:11:45,680 --> 00:11:52,080
Maybe [INAUDIBLE] a little bit.One thing that people have--

183
00:11:52,080 --> 00:11:57,600
has become common practice is to use mixed precision training.

184
00:11:57,600 --> 00:11:59,520
And the actually let me--

185
00:12:02,840 --> 00:12:06,620
OK, let me compile this again.I think these are stale.

186
00:12:06,620 --> 00:12:12,840
So mixed precision training is where some of the computations

187
00:12:12,840 --> 00:12:16,520
use some precision, and for other computations

188
00:12:16,520 --> 00:12:20,760
use other precision.So in general, as a general rule,

189
00:12:20,760 --> 00:12:27,720
bf16 is what you would use for parameters, activations,

190
00:12:27,720 --> 00:12:32,880
and gradients.And for optimizer states, you would use fp32.

191
00:12:32,880 --> 00:12:36,230
And we'll discuss a little bit more about that later.

192
00:12:36,230 --> 00:12:38,530
And to do invoke mixed precision training,

193
00:12:38,530 --> 00:12:40,577
so PyTorch has an AMP library.

194
00:12:40,577 --> 00:12:42,410
We're not going to talk too much about this,

195
00:12:42,410 --> 00:12:45,790
but you basically wrap your code,

196
00:12:45,790 --> 00:12:49,710
and PyTorch has-- the library takes care of it

197
00:12:49,710 --> 00:12:51,710
automatically for you in the sense

198
00:12:51,710 --> 00:12:56,170
that it tries to cast things into a bf16 when it's safe.

199
00:12:56,170 --> 00:12:58,330
So, for example, MatMuls are generally safe.

200
00:12:58,330 --> 00:13:01,170
But if you try to do exponentiation,

201
00:13:01,170 --> 00:13:06,810
then it will try to leave things as fp32. OK.

202
00:13:06,810 --> 00:13:09,030
So we could do bf16, I think is probably

203
00:13:09,030 --> 00:13:11,150
where this class will end.

204
00:13:11,150 --> 00:13:15,850
But if you're feeling very adventurous, you can go farther.

205
00:13:15,850 --> 00:13:19,950
So fp8, this was introduced four years ago.

206
00:13:19,950 --> 00:13:22,610
It actually has been standardized.

207
00:13:22,610 --> 00:13:26,510
So if you look at fp8, there's actually two versions

208
00:13:26,510 --> 00:13:30,390
because depending on if you need more dynamic range or more

209
00:13:30,390 --> 00:13:37,180
resolution. And there's two versions of them.

210
00:13:37,180 --> 00:13:38,930
And so we're not going to talk about that.

211
00:13:38,930 --> 00:13:43,910
But NVIDIA has some--the transformer engine supports fp8.

212
00:13:43,910 --> 00:13:47,470
And most recently, you can actually go down to fp4.

213
00:13:47,470 --> 00:13:50,710
So last year, NVIDIA developed nvfp4,

214
00:13:50,710 --> 00:13:53,193
and there's only 4 bits per value.

215
00:13:53,193 --> 00:13:55,110
So if you just to-- so we're on the same page.

216
00:13:55,110 --> 00:13:56,590
4 bits is not a lot.

217
00:13:56,590 --> 00:14:00,270
I can write all the values down on a single line here

218
00:14:00,270 --> 00:14:05,510
between minus 6 and 6. So that's not very much precision.

219
00:14:05,510 --> 00:14:07,370
Now there's a little bit of a cheat here.

220
00:14:07,370 --> 00:14:11,030
Because if you just naively only use these values,

221
00:14:11,030 --> 00:14:14,950
you're not going to be able to train very well.

222
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:18,950
So what actually this means is that every value

223
00:14:18,950 --> 00:14:23,670
you can have this 4 bits of freedom, but there are blocks.

224
00:14:23,670 --> 00:14:26,410
And each block can be scaled up and down accordingly.

225
00:14:26,410 --> 00:14:28,270
So you can actually represent more values

226
00:14:28,270 --> 00:14:33,350
but you can't represent the full dynamic range

227
00:14:33,350 --> 00:14:35,230
for every single value.

228
00:14:35,230 --> 00:14:39,330
And there is a model released actually this year,

229
00:14:39,330 --> 00:14:42,530
the Nemotron 3 Super, which was trained in fp4,

230
00:14:42,530 --> 00:14:46,270
which I think is pretty cool. OK.

231
00:14:46,270 --> 00:14:50,030
So some of this it's just good to know.

232
00:14:50,030 --> 00:14:52,295
And some of this you can't even touch.

233
00:14:52,295 --> 00:14:53,670
It's not like you create a tensor

234
00:14:53,670 --> 00:14:58,090
and you call it fp-- you can make it fp4.

235
00:14:58,090 --> 00:15:05,370
A lot of this is done under the hood by NVIDIA's software stack.

236
00:15:05,370 --> 00:15:08,730
OK. This was a bit of a long digression,

237
00:15:08,730 --> 00:15:10,990
but it's maybe helpful to appreciate

238
00:15:10,990 --> 00:15:14,710
that the intricacies of precision here.

239
00:15:14,710 --> 00:15:19,110
Any questions before I move on? Yeah. Sorry.

240
00:15:19,110 --> 00:15:21,210
It was a question like, when you have a block,

241
00:15:21,210 --> 00:15:25,050
你正在按那个 block 缩放 tensor 中所有相同的东西。

242
00:15:25,050 --> 00:15:30,780
所以所有这些的比值仍然会处于 fp4 中。是的。

243
00:15:30,780 --> 00:15:32,140
所以问题是，只是——

244
00:15:32,140 --> 00:15:36,220
[INAUDIBLE] 最大值是为了有更高的精度。

245
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:38,260
是的，所以问题是，好，只是

246
00:15:38,260 --> 00:15:39,880
再解释一下 block。

247
00:15:39,880 --> 00:15:41,820
所以你有，比如说一个 block。

248
00:15:41,820 --> 00:15:47,540
在那个 block 内，你可以在 4 位范围内变化。

249
00:15:47,540 --> 00:15:49,460
此外，所有这些值

250
00:15:49,460 --> 00:15:51,460
都可以根据你的缩放因子有多少位

251
00:15:51,460 --> 00:15:54,300
进行放大和缩小。

252
00:15:54,300 --> 00:15:58,460
所以如果你看单个值，

253
00:15:58,460 --> 00:16:01,520
你实际上能得到超过 4 位的动态范围。

254
00:16:01,520 --> 00:16:03,340
但这就像是你不能让

255
00:16:03,340 --> 00:16:06,340
这个值在这边很高，而相邻的值

256
00:16:06,340 --> 00:16:08,100
在这边很低。

257
00:16:08,100 --> 00:16:15,480
一个问题是，那 1 位呢 [INAUDIBLE] 影响 1 位

258
00:16:15,480 --> 00:16:19,500
[INAUDIBLE]？是的，所以显然有很多训练。

259
00:16:19,500 --> 00:16:21,120
所以也许这是个好时机。

260
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:24,220
所以问题是关于 1 位的，因为你不能真的

261
00:16:24,220 --> 00:16:26,300
比那更低。

262
00:16:26,300 --> 00:16:30,600
所以训练和推理之间是有区别的。

263
00:16:30,600 --> 00:16:34,247
所以很多低位的东西，如果我们谈到——

264
00:16:34,247 --> 00:16:36,080
我想我们稍后会谈到量化，

265
00:16:36,080 --> 00:16:41,820
是你在也许甚至 bf16 上训练一个模型。

266
00:16:41,820 --> 00:16:46,060
然后你量化到，比如说，1 位或 2 位。

267
00:16:46,060 --> 00:16:50,920
那比训练一个 1 位语言模型容易得多，

268
00:16:50,920 --> 00:16:54,000
我认为没人做过。

269
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:55,700
也许可能，但我不认为

270
00:16:55,700 --> 00:17:00,180
有人训练过任何可信的东西。好。

271
00:17:00,180 --> 00:17:04,220
那我们继续吧。所以我们谈到了 tensor。

272
00:17:04,220 --> 00:17:06,920
内存计算相当简单，

273
00:17:06,920 --> 00:17:09,020
就是元素数量乘以

274
00:17:09,020 --> 00:17:12,460
每个元素占用多少内存。

275
00:17:12,460 --> 00:17:16,180
所以默认情况下，你在 PyTorch 中创建的 tensor

276
00:17:16,180 --> 00:17:17,760
会在 CPU 上。

277
00:17:20,610 --> 00:17:24,960
当然，你必须——如果你想让事情变快，

278
00:17:24,960 --> 00:17:27,089
你想把它们移到 GPU 上。

279
00:17:27,089 --> 00:17:30,770
实际上，所以有一点——

280
00:17:30,770 --> 00:17:32,658
我对幻灯片有一个小问题

281
00:17:32,658 --> 00:17:34,450
that they were executed on my laptop, which

282
00:17:34,450 --> 00:17:36,350
means that I don't have GPU.

283
00:17:36,350 --> 00:17:40,230
So some of the code I'll just show but not execute.

284
00:17:43,850 --> 00:17:47,990
OK. Let's see. This is maybe not that interesting,

285
00:17:47,990 --> 00:17:52,382
but I think everyone knows how to move tensors to GPU.

286
00:17:52,382 --> 00:17:53,590
But just remember to do that.

287
00:17:53,590 --> 00:17:58,690
Otherwise you won't get your speed-ups.

288
00:17:58,690 --> 00:18:01,030
OK, so we talked about memory of tensors,

289
00:18:01,030 --> 00:18:02,750
which is very straightforward.

290
00:18:02,750 --> 00:18:05,730
Now let's talk about computing with tensors.

291
00:18:05,730 --> 00:18:07,770
So before talking about FLOPs accounting

292
00:18:07,770 --> 00:18:12,050
for the tensor operations, let's take

293
00:18:12,050 --> 00:18:15,410
a little bit of a digression to talk about einops.

294
00:18:15,410 --> 00:18:20,090
So how many of you are familiar used einops before? OK.

295
00:18:20,090 --> 00:18:22,970
So maybe 2/3. OK. Good.

296
00:18:22,970 --> 00:18:26,850
So the motivation behind einops, for those of you

297
00:18:26,850 --> 00:18:32,470
who might not be indoctrinated is, it's very easy to mess up.

298
00:18:32,470 --> 00:18:38,210
Or I find it very confusing to look at code such as this.

299
00:18:38,210 --> 00:18:41,490
And you have x and y.

300
00:18:41,490 --> 00:18:44,450
And then you have transpose minus 2 and minus 1.

301
00:18:44,450 --> 00:18:50,930
And you're trying to figure out what minus 2 and minus 1 is.

302
00:18:50,930 --> 00:18:55,970
And so this is maybe the motivation

303
00:18:55,970 --> 00:19:02,010
for using variable and names rather than indices.

304
00:19:02,010 --> 00:19:05,210
So einops is a library for manipulating tensors

305
00:19:05,210 --> 00:19:06,590
where the dimensions are named.

306
00:19:06,590 --> 00:19:10,390
And this is inspired by Einstein summation notation.

307
00:19:10,390 --> 00:19:12,810
There's a nice tutorial which you can go through.

308
00:19:12,810 --> 00:19:17,730
I'm just going to cover some of the basics.

309
00:19:17,730 --> 00:19:20,250
So basically, the way to think about einsum

310
00:19:20,250 --> 00:19:25,270
is a generalized matrix multiplication with good bookkeeping.

311
00:19:25,270 --> 00:19:31,690
So here's an example. So I have a matrix 3 by 4.

312
00:19:31,690 --> 00:19:33,290
I have a 4 by 3 matrix.

313
00:19:33,290 --> 00:19:39,470
And if I do the MatMul, this is actually pretty nice.

314
00:19:39,470 --> 00:19:41,650
It's pretty easy to understand.

315
00:19:41,650 --> 00:19:47,450
In einops, basically you say x has two dimensions, the row

316
00:19:47,450 --> 00:19:50,030
and the column, which I'm going to name seq1 and hidden.

317
00:19:50,030 --> 00:19:51,070
I'm going to have y.

318
00:19:51,070 --> 00:19:54,810
That's a matrix which has also rows and columns, which I'm

319
00:19:54,810 --> 00:19:58,970
going to name hidden and seq2.

320
00:19:58,970 --> 00:20:03,410
And I'm going to produce tensor or here

321
00:20:03,410 --> 00:20:08,810
a matrix, where the dimensions are indexed by seq1 and seq2.

322
00:20:08,810 --> 00:20:11,450
And anything that is not mentioned here,

323
00:20:11,450 --> 00:20:14,330
the hidden gets summed out.

324
00:20:14,330 --> 00:20:17,890
So the way this works is I'm going to sum over--

325
00:20:17,890 --> 00:20:22,680
enumerate over all possible values of all the variables

326
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:28,080
that occur here, which are seq1's hidden and seq2.

327
00:20:28,080 --> 00:20:33,420
And I'm going to basically index into x, index into y,

328
00:20:33,420 --> 00:20:35,880
multiply them and dump them, accumulate them

329
00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:40,480
into the result z sub seq1 and seq2.

330
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:47,340
All right. So you say this is--come on.

331
00:20:47,340 --> 00:20:51,520
This is much easier than this, right?

332
00:20:51,520 --> 00:20:53,380
OK, let's try a more complicated example.

333
00:20:53,380 --> 00:20:58,920
So here's the example I showed before. Now we have a tensor.

334
00:20:58,920 --> 00:21:03,120
It's 2 by 3 by 4, another tensor 2 by 3 by 4.

335
00:21:03,120 --> 00:21:08,720
And if I were doing things the old way, basically what

336
00:21:08,720 --> 00:21:12,760
I'm doing is transposing these last two.

337
00:21:12,760 --> 00:21:18,000
And then because at implicitly batches

338
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:22,720
the dimensions that are not the MatMul dimensions, then

339
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:26,400
you get the answer. So you have to reason about this a bit.

340
00:21:26,400 --> 00:21:29,000
Einops makes this very clear.

341
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,040
It says there's a batch dimension.

342
00:21:31,040 --> 00:21:34,220
There's seq1 hidden, seq2 hidden.

343
00:21:34,220 --> 00:21:38,640
And I'm just going to produce batch seq1 and seq2.

344
00:21:38,640 --> 00:21:43,040
And notice that there's no transpose because I just--

345
00:21:43,040 --> 00:21:45,960
in some sense I've done the transpose by the naming.

346
00:21:45,960 --> 00:21:50,240
If I had hidden and seq2, then it would be no transpose.

347
00:21:50,240 --> 00:21:52,060
I always get confused by transposes.

348
00:21:52,060 --> 00:21:54,440
And the fact I don't have to think about transposing it

349
00:21:54,440 --> 00:21:59,400
makes me happy. OK.

350
00:21:59,400 --> 00:22:05,280
So if you want to get fancy, you can say, well, batch--

351
00:22:05,280 --> 00:22:07,720
I'm just going to replace with dot, dot, dot.

352
00:22:07,720 --> 00:22:13,280
And this means that if I had, let's say, rank

353
00:22:13,280 --> 00:22:17,460
10 tensor with eight different batching dimensions,

354
00:22:17,460 --> 00:22:20,640
I can just write dot dot, dot without enumerating all of them.

355
00:22:20,640 --> 00:22:23,240
And this comes up in language modeling

356
00:22:23,240 --> 00:22:25,280
because you might have a batch dimension.

357
00:22:25,280 --> 00:22:26,780
You might have a sequence dimension.

358
00:22:26,780 --> 00:22:28,260
You might have a head dimension.

359
00:22:28,260 --> 00:22:30,680
And you're trying to do this matrix operation

360
00:22:30,680 --> 00:22:31,740
for all of them.

361
00:22:31,740 --> 00:22:36,902
And you might not want to have to just worry about this.

362
00:22:36,902 --> 00:22:39,360
And the nice thing is that you can write modular code where

363
00:22:39,360 --> 00:22:42,080
you can write this dot, dot, dot without worrying

364
00:22:42,080 --> 00:22:45,700
about even the shape of the tensor that comes in.

365
00:22:49,670 --> 00:22:55,560
All right. So that's einsum which is in the einops library.

366
00:22:55,560 --> 00:22:58,280
There's a reduce.

367
00:22:58,280 --> 00:23:03,320
So this is a generalization of sum, mean, max, and min.

368
00:23:03,320 --> 00:23:09,360
So for example, if you have, let's say, this tensor

369
00:23:09,360 --> 00:23:14,510
and you want to sum according to dimension of dim minus 1,

370
00:23:14,510 --> 00:23:19,710
which means sum along the last dimension here.

371
00:23:19,710 --> 00:23:23,490
So again, I don't like this notation.

372
00:23:23,490 --> 00:23:25,870
But what you can do is call reduce.

373
00:23:25,870 --> 00:23:28,710
And basically what you say is that there's

374
00:23:28,710 --> 00:23:30,010
some batching dimensions.

375
00:23:30,010 --> 00:23:33,510
In this case, it would be these first two.

376
00:23:33,510 --> 00:23:36,350
And then there's some hidden dimension

377
00:23:36,350 --> 00:23:38,270
which doesn't appear on the right side, which

378
00:23:38,270 --> 00:23:40,030
means that it gets summed.

379
00:23:40,030 --> 00:23:42,950
And here, I put sum, which means that the aggregation reduction

380
00:23:42,950 --> 00:23:44,050
operation is sum.

381
00:23:44,050 --> 00:23:50,070
But you can replace it with mean or max or min. Yeah.

382
00:23:50,070 --> 00:23:51,770
Is there some speed-up to this?

383
00:23:51,770 --> 00:23:53,870
This basically reduces to the same type

384
00:23:53,870 --> 00:23:55,170
of primitive operations.

385
00:23:55,170 --> 00:23:57,448
You can think about it as just sugar.

386
00:23:57,448 --> 00:23:58,490
So it should be the same.

387
00:24:03,170 --> 00:24:06,890
OK. So the final thing I'll talk about is rearrange.

388
00:24:06,890 --> 00:24:10,270
So this is, I think, a pretty powerful tool.

389
00:24:10,270 --> 00:24:13,830
I think it will come up in an assignment once.

390
00:24:13,830 --> 00:24:15,430
So sometimes you have a dimension

391
00:24:15,430 --> 00:24:18,830
that actually represents two dimensions,

392
00:24:18,830 --> 00:24:21,085
and you want to operate on one of them.

393
00:24:21,085 --> 00:24:23,710
And the reason this happens is that sometimes you have a matrix

394
00:24:23,710 --> 00:24:25,750
and you flatten it.

395
00:24:25,750 --> 00:24:28,490
And then you want to maybe unflatten and flatten.

396
00:24:28,490 --> 00:24:32,390
So this is the way that it works here.

397
00:24:32,390 --> 00:24:36,230
So imagine I have a matrix 3 by 8

398
00:24:36,230 --> 00:24:44,470
but where this dimension, 8 actually represents a 2

399
00:24:44,470 --> 00:24:46,990
by 4 matrix.

400
00:24:46,990 --> 00:24:53,430
So I want to multiply that 2 by 4 matrix by this 4 by 4 matrix.

401
00:24:53,430 --> 00:24:59,590
所以我接下来要做的，实际上是调用rearrange。

402
00:24:59,590 --> 00:25:04,730
而我在这里做的是说，看，

403
00:25:04,730 --> 00:25:06,830
这是一些批次维度，

404
00:25:06,830 --> 00:25:10,910
这里它对应的就是这里的第一个元素。

405
00:25:10,910 --> 00:25:15,230
然后在这里，我用括号表示这个h实际上

406
00:25:15,230 --> 00:25:19,390
代表heads和hidden1的乘积，

407
00:25:19,390 --> 00:25:21,470
这里我必须——显然有多种方式

408
00:25:21,470 --> 00:25:23,610
来分解它。可以是2乘4，4乘2。

409
00:25:23,610 --> 00:25:25,390
所以我说heads的数量是2，

410
00:25:25,390 --> 00:25:27,990
这意味着hidden1是4。

411
00:25:27,990 --> 00:25:34,390
然后我可以把它拆成两个维度，heads

412
00:25:34,390 --> 00:25:38,770
和hidden。好。这样就创建了这个。

413
00:25:38,770 --> 00:25:45,190
所以之前x看起来像这样，现在x看起来像这样。

414
00:25:45,190 --> 00:25:50,270
所以我想这可能有点难看清。

415
00:25:50,270 --> 00:25:55,670
然后你可以对w执行你的操作变换。

416
00:25:55,670 --> 00:25:58,070
这就是我们之前见过的，

417
00:25:58,070 --> 00:26:02,230
你有一个标准的MatMul，

418
00:26:02,230 --> 00:26:07,150
这里x有一些批次维度。

419
00:26:07,150 --> 00:26:12,540
这是hidden乘以hidden2。

420
00:26:12,540 --> 00:26:14,980
然后一旦你完成了这个变换，

421
00:26:14,980 --> 00:26:18,840
你就可以rearrange回来。这很直接。

422
00:26:18,840 --> 00:26:20,920
你基本上是看两个维度，

423
00:26:20,920 --> 00:26:24,800
然后你可以把它们组合成一个维度。

424
00:26:30,220 --> 00:26:32,900
是的。你能把一个二维的东西

425
00:26:32,900 --> 00:26:35,840
塑造成一维的吗，不是有两种方式吗？

426
00:26:35,840 --> 00:26:38,080
比如，你可以按行主序或列主序来做？

427
00:26:38,080 --> 00:26:40,580
是的，所以问题是如果你有一个一维的东西

428
00:26:40,580 --> 00:26:43,687
然后你把它变成——抱歉，你有一个二维的东西

429
00:26:43,687 --> 00:26:45,520
然后你把它变成一维的东西，

430
00:26:45,520 --> 00:26:47,600
你用哪种方式做？

431
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:52,620
嗯，你做的顺序是由这里的顺序指定的。好。

432
00:26:52,620 --> 00:26:53,120
是的。

433
00:26:57,080 --> 00:27:00,900
所以有时我觉得需要一点时间来适应，

434
00:27:00,900 --> 00:27:04,680
但这很值得，因为一旦你有了einsum，

435
00:27:04,680 --> 00:27:07,360
你就会以不同的方式思考。

436
00:27:07,360 --> 00:27:10,340
所有的转置和归约，所有这些，它只是

437
00:27:10,340 --> 00:27:13,300
它变得更流畅。

438
00:27:13,300 --> 00:27:17,040
你必须更深入地思考这些更定制的原语。

439
00:27:19,740 --> 00:27:24,700
好的。好。我们要用einops。

440
00:27:24,700 --> 00:27:27,460
我们稍后会看到一点。

441
00:27:27,460 --> 00:27:30,220
那么现在我们回到资源核算的问题。

442
00:27:30,220 --> 00:27:32,120
我有一些张量。

443
00:27:32,120 --> 00:27:34,500
我们已经讨论过它们如何占用内存。

444
00:27:34,500 --> 00:27:36,980
那么它们消耗多少计算量呢？

445
00:27:36,980 --> 00:27:41,980
我们用来衡量计算成本的标准

446
00:27:41,980 --> 00:27:45,580
是FLOPs的数量。一个FLOP是一次浮点运算。

447
00:27:45,580 --> 00:27:48,980
我们假设它是一次基本运算，比如加法

448
00:27:48,980 --> 00:27:50,740
或乘法。

449
00:27:50,740 --> 00:27:53,485
当然，GPU还能做其他事情。

450
00:27:53,485 --> 00:27:54,860
但大多数情况下，我们只是

451
00:27:54,860 --> 00:27:59,100
忽略它们，因为这些才是基础，

452
00:27:59,100 --> 00:28:03,260
会占用你大部分时间。

453
00:28:03,260 --> 00:28:08,870
让我很在意的一点是，如果我说FLOPs这个词，

454
00:28:08,870 --> 00:28:11,570
它实际上是有歧义的。

455
00:28:11,570 --> 00:28:15,050
有FLOPs，指的是

456
00:28:15,050 --> 00:28:16,930
浮点运算的数量，

457
00:28:16,930 --> 00:28:20,250
通常写作FLOPs，小写s。

458
00:28:20,250 --> 00:28:22,490
这是已完成的计算量的度量。

459
00:28:22,490 --> 00:28:26,610
然后还有FLOP/s，即每秒浮点运算

460
00:28:26,610 --> 00:28:28,410
次数。

461
00:28:28,410 --> 00:28:30,850
有时候也很容易混淆地写成FLOPS

462
00:28:30,850 --> 00:28:34,330
大写S，但我会一直写/s

463
00:28:34,330 --> 00:28:38,330
来明确这是在衡量硬件的速度。

464
00:28:38,330 --> 00:28:45,750
所以如果你看到H100有800、989万亿次浮点运算，

465
00:28:45,750 --> 00:28:46,830
那是第二种。

466
00:28:46,830 --> 00:28:52,710
而当我说GPT-3用了325或者其他数字的FLOPs时，

467
00:28:52,710 --> 00:28:58,410
那是第一种。先把这一点说清楚。

468
00:28:58,410 --> 00:29:00,990
先给大家一个数量级的概念。

469
00:29:00,990 --> 00:29:06,550
所以当我说1e22或23或25的FLOPs时，

470
00:29:06,550 --> 00:29:09,570
这些隐式地指的是计算量

471
00:29:09,570 --> 00:29:13,050
或者某些模型的规模。

472
00:29:13,050 --> 00:29:17,890
所以如果你看H100——

473
00:29:17,890 --> 00:29:23,530
如果你看这份光鲜的规格表——

474
00:29:23,530 --> 00:29:27,050
其实不在这页上。算了。

475
00:29:27,050 --> 00:29:29,370
有一份规格表，我会告诉你

476
00:29:29,370 --> 00:29:40,650
对于bf16，FLOP/s的数量是1979。

477
00:29:40,650 --> 00:29:42,650
然后你去实际测试一下，

478
00:29:42,650 --> 00:29:44,650
你会发现等等，那并不是

479
00:29:44,650 --> 00:29:47,950
我实际得到的结果。然后你去读细则。

480
00:29:47,950 --> 00:29:50,470
有一个脚注说这是在稀疏性条件下的结果。

481
00:29:50,470 --> 00:29:54,770
所以稀疏矩阵和稠密矩阵都是 2。

482
00:29:54,770 --> 00:29:57,630
所以你总是要把这些数字除以 2。

483
00:29:57,630 --> 00:30:01,610
所以这就是为什么你看到这些除以 2。好的。

484
00:30:04,370 --> 00:30:07,010
所以这让我们可以——

485
00:30:09,665 --> 00:30:12,370
只是为了直观理解，假设你有 8 块 H100

486
00:30:12,370 --> 00:30:15,650
所以这里有一个节点运行两周。

487
00:30:15,650 --> 00:30:23,730
那就是 8 乘以两周内的秒数。

488
00:30:23,730 --> 00:30:26,830
其实，这看起来是一周。好的，没问题。

489
00:30:26,830 --> 00:30:30,870
它是一周乘以你每秒获得的 FLOPs 数。

490
00:30:30,870 --> 00:30:36,150
所以大约是 5e21。好的。

491
00:30:36,150 --> 00:30:37,810
所以这只是为了建立直观理解

492
00:30:37,810 --> 00:30:40,330
关于某些类型硬件的 FLOPs 数

493
00:30:40,330 --> 00:30:44,850
以及某些类型模型需要多少 FLOPs。

494
00:30:44,850 --> 00:30:50,490
这没什么复杂的。只是数学和粗略估算。好的。

495
00:30:50,490 --> 00:30:54,850
所以现在让我们做一些更机械的事情。

496
00:30:54,850 --> 00:30:59,600
所以假设你有一个线性模型。

497
00:30:59,600 --> 00:31:04,760
事实证明，很多计算 FLOPs 的方法

498
00:31:04,760 --> 00:31:08,740
实际上核心就是线性矩阵乘法。

499
00:31:08,740 --> 00:31:11,740
所以这其实并没有太大的通用性损失。

500
00:31:11,740 --> 00:31:14,200
所以你有 n 个点。每个点是 d 维的。

501
00:31:14,200 --> 00:31:21,240
我们将把每个 d 维向量

502
00:31:21,240 --> 00:31:25,260
映射到一个 k 维输出。

503
00:31:25,260 --> 00:31:28,560
所以 B 是点的数量。

504
00:31:28,560 --> 00:31:31,160
D 是维度数，输入维度，

505
00:31:31,160 --> 00:31:33,480
K 是输出维度。好的。

506
00:31:33,480 --> 00:31:39,680
所以让我们构造一些 x，即数据矩阵

507
00:31:39,680 --> 00:31:42,720
B 乘 D。权重矩阵是 D 乘 K。

508
00:31:42,720 --> 00:31:47,760
当你做矩阵乘法时，问题是这有多少

509
00:31:47,760 --> 00:31:52,480
FLOPs。事实证明这

510
00:31:52,480 --> 00:31:59,280
基本上是三个维度的乘积的 2 倍。

511
00:31:59,280 --> 00:32:03,120
理解这一点的方式是我们

512
00:32:03,120 --> 00:32:10,080
对每个三元组有一次乘法，然后还有一次

513
00:32:10,080 --> 00:32:14,920
加法。所以有一个减 1，因为如果你

514
00:32:14,920 --> 00:32:20,860
不必实际相加，就像这种情况下 D 减 1 次。

515
00:32:20,860 --> 00:32:24,840
但让我们忽略这一点。好的。我会回到这个问题。

516
00:32:24,840 --> 00:32:27,400
如果这有点快，我想还有

517
00:32:27,400 --> 00:32:31,600
另一种推导方法。

518
00:32:31,600 --> 00:32:34,820
那么其他操作的 FLOPs 呢？

519
00:32:34,820 --> 00:32:39,660
所以逐元素操作就是矩阵的大小。

520
00:32:39,660 --> 00:32:40,980
我想这相当清楚。

521
00:32:40,980 --> 00:32:44,000
So addition also requires m n FLOPs.

522
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:46,480
So in general, no other operation

523
00:32:46,480 --> 00:32:50,120
you'll encounter as expensive as matrix multiplication

524
00:32:50,120 --> 00:32:51,580
for large enough matrices.

525
00:32:51,580 --> 00:32:53,640
So in general, we're just going to focus

526
00:32:53,640 --> 00:32:57,760
on what MatMuls are doing, with important caveat of when

527
00:32:57,760 --> 00:33:00,200
we talk about memory.

528
00:33:00,200 --> 00:33:04,720
Just from interest, there are some other algorithms

529
00:33:04,720 --> 00:33:06,420
for doing matrix multiplication.

530
00:33:06,420 --> 00:33:10,040
Is this only for just doing it normally?

531
00:33:10,040 --> 00:33:12,200
So the question is that there are other algorithms

532
00:33:12,200 --> 00:33:13,860
of doing matrix multiplication.

533
00:33:13,860 --> 00:33:17,360
You mean like sub cubic algorithms?

534
00:33:17,360 --> 00:33:20,040
So in general, that's--

535
00:33:20,040 --> 00:33:21,960
the optimization, the algorithms that people

536
00:33:21,960 --> 00:33:24,400
are going to explore for multiple matrix multiplications

537
00:33:24,400 --> 00:33:27,560
are going to be much more about how you co-design

538
00:33:27,560 --> 00:33:29,240
with the systems, rather than these more

539
00:33:29,240 --> 00:33:34,840
asymptotic algorithms.

540
00:33:34,840 --> 00:33:37,120
We're considering additional multiplication

541
00:33:37,120 --> 00:33:40,960
in the same way is not possible to do division more efficiently

542
00:33:40,960 --> 00:33:42,520
than multiplication.

543
00:33:42,520 --> 00:33:45,960
Yeah, so I think the way the hardware is built,

544
00:33:45,960 --> 00:33:48,880
the two are basically the same.

545
00:33:48,880 --> 00:33:51,610
But yeah, intuitively it seems like I can do addition faster

546
00:33:51,610 --> 00:33:53,130
than I do multiplication.

547
00:33:53,130 --> 00:33:59,530
But the way the hardware is kind of the same.

548
00:33:59,530 --> 00:34:01,830
So you can think about this MatMul

549
00:34:01,830 --> 00:34:07,090
as B is the number of data points.

550
00:34:07,090 --> 00:34:09,370
And D K is the number of parameters.

551
00:34:09,370 --> 00:34:14,510
So remember x is B by D and w is D by K.

552
00:34:14,510 --> 00:34:18,230
So another way to think about this formula

553
00:34:18,230 --> 00:34:23,230
is that the number of FLOPs in order to do

554
00:34:23,230 --> 00:34:26,389
a forward pass of this linear matrix

555
00:34:26,389 --> 00:34:30,830
is actually 2 times the number of tokens or data points

556
00:34:30,830 --> 00:34:33,150
times the number of parameters.

557
00:34:33,150 --> 00:34:36,630
So it turns out that this actually

558
00:34:36,630 --> 00:34:38,429
generalizes to transformers, which

559
00:34:38,429 --> 00:34:41,110
if you remember the 6 times n times d formula,

560
00:34:41,110 --> 00:34:46,270
we can see the shape of that forming.

561
00:34:46,270 --> 00:34:49,750
So unfortunately these calculations

562
00:34:49,750 --> 00:34:51,670
are not going to be very meaningful

563
00:34:51,670 --> 00:34:54,050
because I'm doing this on CPU.

564
00:34:54,050 --> 00:34:58,830
But I'll just walk through the code here.

565
00:34:58,830 --> 00:35:04,830
So what we've done so far is measured FLOPs.

566
00:35:04,830 --> 00:35:07,922
So this is independent of hardware.

567
00:35:07,922 --> 00:35:09,630
It's just like the number of calculations

568
00:35:09,630 --> 00:35:12,030
you need to do for your model.

569
00:35:12,030 --> 00:35:14,190
So now the question is, how long does it actually

570
00:35:14,190 --> 00:35:18,790
take on hardware? So one way to find out is you just time it.

571
00:35:18,790 --> 00:35:21,410
So in this class, I think in the few lectures,

572
00:35:21,410 --> 00:35:24,990
we're going to talk more about benchmarking.

573
00:35:24,990 --> 00:35:27,270
But here's a little preview.

574
00:35:27,270 --> 00:35:33,230
So in general, when you time especially on GPU,

575
00:35:33,230 --> 00:35:37,150
you have to call cuda synchronize

576
00:35:37,150 --> 00:35:41,050
to make sure that-- because the GPU is running asynchronously,

577
00:35:41,050 --> 00:35:46,110
you want to make sure that you have this synchronization point.

578
00:35:46,110 --> 00:35:47,630
And then you perform the operation.

579
00:35:47,630 --> 00:35:52,950
And after operation, then you also

580
00:35:52,950 --> 00:35:55,070
have to have the synchronization barrier.

581
00:35:55,070 --> 00:35:57,810
If you omit this, you're going to find that wow,

582
00:35:57,810 --> 00:35:59,092
your timings are really fast.

583
00:35:59,092 --> 00:36:01,050
And that's because this is a non-blocking call.

584
00:36:01,050 --> 00:36:04,750
It just returns. And often it's general good practice

585
00:36:04,750 --> 00:36:07,770
to try this multiple times and take the average.

586
00:36:11,958 --> 00:36:20,070
So the actual FLOPs per second is basically the number of FLOPs

587
00:36:20,070 --> 00:36:24,990
you did times the time that you recorded on your hardware.

588
00:36:24,990 --> 00:36:29,990
And so remember, there's also another number, which

589
00:36:29,990 --> 00:36:32,590
is the GPU has a spec sheet.

590
00:36:32,590 --> 00:36:36,330
Let's see if I can pull it up on this link.

591
00:36:39,030 --> 00:36:41,050
I feel like maybe I have the wrong link.

592
00:36:41,050 --> 00:36:42,790
I'll have to fix that after class.

593
00:36:42,790 --> 00:36:50,160
--that it gives you some number of FLOPs,

594
00:36:50,160 --> 00:36:54,820
which was 989 teraflops.

595
00:36:54,820 --> 00:36:59,260
And so in general, the number of actual FLOPs per second

596
00:36:59,260 --> 00:37:02,760
is going to be different from the promised FLOPs per second.

597
00:37:07,500 --> 00:37:12,100
OK. Oops, sorry.

598
00:37:12,100 --> 00:37:15,660
And the way to directly think about the discrepancy

599
00:37:15,660 --> 00:37:20,380
is something called Model FLOPs Utilization, or MFU.

600
00:37:20,380 --> 00:37:24,500
And the definition of MFU is the actual FLOPs

601
00:37:24,500 --> 00:37:27,940
每秒除以承诺的FLOPs每秒。

602
00:37:27,940 --> 00:37:32,740
这里忽略了通信

603
00:37:32,740 --> 00:37:34,220
和其他开销。

604
00:37:34,220 --> 00:37:39,140
所以你基本上取实际值除以承诺值。

605
00:37:39,140 --> 00:37:44,300
所以一般来说，你很少能得到超过——

606
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:49,580
我的意思是，你——是的，你永远得不到超过

607
00:37:49,580 --> 00:37:52,060
你被承诺的值。通常你会得到更少。

608
00:37:52,060 --> 00:37:57,980
一般来说，如果现代模型的MFU能达到0.5，

609
00:37:57,980 --> 00:38:01,140
你应该对自己相当满意了。

610
00:38:01,140 --> 00:38:03,960
如果你只是做一个纯粹的矩阵乘法，

611
00:38:03,960 --> 00:38:09,180
你可能能达到0.8甚至更高，

612
00:38:09,180 --> 00:38:12,500
但你通常达不到那么高。

613
00:38:12,500 --> 00:38:16,883
有时候如果你真的——某件事

614
00:38:16,883 --> 00:38:18,300
真的出错了，你会得到

615
00:38:18,300 --> 00:38:22,700
像0.1这样的结果，这意味着你应该做点什么。好。

616
00:38:22,700 --> 00:38:27,300
所以每当你写模型时，你可以计算。

617
00:38:27,300 --> 00:38:30,100
你现在知道如何通过结合

618
00:38:30,100 --> 00:38:32,420
计算模型需要执行的逻辑FLOPs数量，

619
00:38:32,420 --> 00:38:39,620
然后查看墙上时钟

620
00:38:39,620 --> 00:38:42,497
时间并基本上相除来计算MFU。是的。

621
00:38:42,497 --> 00:38:47,980
那边有问题吗？哦。[听不清]

622
00:38:49,370 --> 00:38:53,210
是的。那么承诺——承诺的FLOPs数字是什么？

623
00:38:53,210 --> 00:38:57,570
那在规格表中，它已经

624
00:38:57,570 --> 00:39:02,930
除以2了，那个989的数字。

625
00:39:02,930 --> 00:39:06,770
然后在此基础上，一般来说你只能得到其中的0.5

626
00:39:06,770 --> 00:39:10,370
取决于你的计算。

627
00:39:10,370 --> 00:39:15,550
[听不清]你得到50%的MFU？

628
00:39:15,550 --> 00:39:18,455
是的，那么为什么你只得到50%的MFU？

629
00:39:18,455 --> 00:39:19,830
实际上，这是个好问题。

630
00:39:19,830 --> 00:39:22,590
我会在讨论内存瓶颈时再回来讲这个。

631
00:39:26,226 --> 00:39:31,890
好。所以这里总结一下，矩阵乘法

632
00:39:31,890 --> 00:39:38,150
通常主导了计算。这是设计使然。

633
00:39:41,862 --> 00:39:46,570
而每秒的FLOPs数量取决于硬件。

634
00:39:46,570 --> 00:39:51,090
所以更好的硬件带来更多的FLOPs

635
00:39:51,090 --> 00:39:54,410
并且还取决于数据类型。

636
00:39:54,410 --> 00:39:58,090
这意味着如果你查看规格表，

637
00:39:58,090 --> 00:40:00,270
不同的数据类型会有不同的FLOPs。

638
00:40:00,270 --> 00:40:02,430
如果你现在尝试使用float32，

639
00:40:02,430 --> 00:40:05,690
它会非常非常慢，因为他们并没有真正

640
00:40:05,690 --> 00:40:07,750
为此工作负载进行优化。

641
00:40:07,750 --> 00:40:16,010
Whereas now bf16 or fp8 are going to be much faster.

642
00:40:16,010 --> 00:40:18,770
And MFU-- now you know what MFU is.

643
00:40:18,770 --> 00:40:22,590
It's the actual FLOPs divided by promised FLOPs.

644
00:40:27,330 --> 00:40:32,755
All right. So to go back to the question of why is MFU 0.5.

645
00:40:32,755 --> 00:40:34,130
And to understand that, I'm going

646
00:40:34,130 --> 00:40:37,610
to have to introduce this idea of arithmetic intensity.

647
00:40:37,610 --> 00:40:42,050
And the reason is that, well, it's not just

648
00:40:42,050 --> 00:40:46,410
doing a bunch of MatMuls and then you're

649
00:40:46,410 --> 00:40:50,130
done and looking at how long the MatMuls take.

650
00:40:50,130 --> 00:40:56,050
This is my very cartoon version of what hardware looks like.

651
00:40:56,050 --> 00:40:58,930
You have high bandwidth memory.

652
00:40:58,930 --> 00:41:02,110
And then you have where the compute cores are,

653
00:41:02,110 --> 00:41:08,050
the accelerator chips are. And then how do you compute?

654
00:41:08,050 --> 00:41:13,490
Well, you have to send your inputs, your matrices--

655
00:41:13,490 --> 00:41:15,090
the tensors are sitting down here--

656
00:41:15,090 --> 00:41:17,050
from the memory to the accelerator.

657
00:41:17,050 --> 00:41:22,970
You do the computation, and then you send it back.

658
00:41:22,970 --> 00:41:26,010
So if you want to measure how long this takes,

659
00:41:26,010 --> 00:41:27,790
this depends on two things.

660
00:41:27,790 --> 00:41:30,570
One is the accelerator speed, which

661
00:41:30,570 --> 00:41:34,267
is what we've talked about just now.

662
00:41:34,267 --> 00:41:36,600
But the other thing that matters is the memory bandwidth

663
00:41:36,600 --> 00:41:39,760
of your hardware, which we haven't talked about.

664
00:41:39,760 --> 00:41:42,420
And if you look at the spec sheet,

665
00:41:42,420 --> 00:41:48,960
we talked about how the FLOPs per second was 1979e12 divided

666
00:41:48,960 --> 00:41:50,040
by 2.

667
00:41:50,040 --> 00:41:52,700
And the bytes per second, which is the memory bandwidth,

668
00:41:52,700 --> 00:41:57,400
this is 3.3 terabytes per second.

669
00:41:57,400 --> 00:42:04,200
And remember why we were looking at memory,

670
00:42:04,200 --> 00:42:07,040
how much things took to store.

671
00:42:07,040 --> 00:42:11,097
It's most obviously that, well, if you

672
00:42:11,097 --> 00:42:12,680
have a model that's too big it doesn't

673
00:42:12,680 --> 00:42:15,360
fit in your memory, that's not going to be fun.

674
00:42:15,360 --> 00:42:18,960
But also it turns out that memory--

675
00:42:18,960 --> 00:42:21,300
you need to move this memory, which takes time.

676
00:42:21,300 --> 00:42:23,960
So actually the size of how large things

677
00:42:23,960 --> 00:42:27,560
actually influences speed as well. OK.

678
00:42:27,560 --> 00:42:31,600
So I'm going to talk through some operations,

679
00:42:31,600 --> 00:42:33,200
and I'm going to basically compute

680
00:42:33,200 --> 00:42:35,840
how long things are going to take and introduce

681
00:42:35,840 --> 00:42:38,040
这个算术强度的概念。

682
00:42:38,040 --> 00:42:45,980
好，假设我有一个百万维的 bf16 向量。

683
00:42:45,980 --> 00:42:49,900
然后我要对它计算一个 ReLU。

684
00:42:49,900 --> 00:42:52,400
记住 ReLU 就是取 x

685
00:42:52,400 --> 00:42:58,320
和 0 的逐元素最大值，作用于整个向量。

686
00:42:58,320 --> 00:43:02,680
所以我计算两件事，一是

687
00:43:02,680 --> 00:43:10,160
移动的字节数。所以我要读入 x——

688
00:43:10,160 --> 00:43:14,720
把它复制到加速器里。

689
00:43:14,720 --> 00:43:18,240
每一个都是 2

690
00:43:18,240 --> 00:43:21,760
因为 bf16 是每个浮点数 2 字节，乘以 n 个浮点数。

691
00:43:21,760 --> 00:43:25,040
所以是 2n。然后我要把 y 写回去。

692
00:43:25,040 --> 00:43:27,580
所以又是 2n。好。

693
00:43:27,580 --> 00:43:30,040
这就是需要移动的字节数。

694
00:43:30,040 --> 00:43:32,880
那么做了多少 FLOPs？

695
00:43:32,880 --> 00:43:37,600
每个元素，我只是拿它和零比较，

696
00:43:37,600 --> 00:43:41,880
就这样。所以是 n 次比较。

697
00:43:41,880 --> 00:43:46,500
那么现在看通信时间，

698
00:43:46,500 --> 00:43:52,880
就是我需要移动的字节数除以

699
00:43:52,880 --> 00:43:55,600
移动的速度。

700
00:43:55,600 --> 00:44:01,000
这给出时间，是 1e-6 秒。

701
00:44:01,000 --> 00:44:02,740
那计算时间呢？

702
00:44:02,740 --> 00:44:05,320
就是 FLOPs 除以每秒 FLOPs。

703
00:44:05,320 --> 00:44:10,760
所以是 1e-9。好。

704
00:44:10,760 --> 00:44:14,320
还有一个重要的假设

705
00:44:14,320 --> 00:44:18,680
我们通常试图保持，就是重叠通信

706
00:44:18,680 --> 00:44:21,120
和计算。我们会在

707
00:44:21,120 --> 00:44:23,200
更深入讨论 GPU 时多谈这个。

708
00:44:23,200 --> 00:44:26,320
但这个想法是，在这种情况下

709
00:44:26,320 --> 00:44:32,010
我们不会坐在这里等东西移动。

710
00:44:32,010 --> 00:44:34,450
一旦它们到了，我们就开始计算，

711
00:44:34,450 --> 00:44:36,950
然后把它们移回去。

712
00:44:36,950 --> 00:44:41,030
所以这个移动以及计算

713
00:44:41,030 --> 00:44:43,670
是同时发生的。

714
00:44:43,670 --> 00:44:46,910
所以数学上，我们只是

715
00:44:46,910 --> 00:44:49,665
假设总时间是两者的最大值，

716
00:44:49,665 --> 00:44:51,790
因为我们假设可以完美地

717
00:44:51,790 --> 00:44:52,390
重叠它们。

718
00:44:52,390 --> 00:44:54,210
实践中，它不会完美重叠。

719
00:44:54,210 --> 00:44:55,627
会有一些开销。

720
00:44:55,627 --> 00:44:58,770
但这对现在来说足够了。好。

721
00:44:58,770 --> 00:45:05,310
So the total time, as we see here, is 1e-6. OK.

722
00:45:05,310 --> 00:45:08,750
So if you ask what is the bottleneck here?

723
00:45:08,750 --> 00:45:12,230
So when the communication time is greater than the computation

724
00:45:12,230 --> 00:45:15,460
time, then we call it the algorithm memory bound,

725
00:45:15,460 --> 00:45:17,710
because you're spending most of your time just waiting

726
00:45:17,710 --> 00:45:21,173
for bits to show up. And when the computation time is greater

727
00:45:21,173 --> 00:45:22,590
than the communication time, that's

728
00:45:22,590 --> 00:45:24,830
compute bound, because then you're

729
00:45:24,830 --> 00:45:29,270
actually-- your bottleneck is actually doing the compute.

730
00:45:29,270 --> 00:45:31,650
And so in this case, what is ReLU?

731
00:45:34,390 --> 00:45:36,290
Rectified Linear Unit. Oh, sorry.

732
00:45:36,290 --> 00:45:42,110
Is it memory bound or compute bound? Memory bound. Yeah.

733
00:45:44,870 --> 00:45:49,150
And it's clear, because the compute is way less

734
00:45:49,150 --> 00:45:50,490
than the communication.

735
00:45:53,310 --> 00:45:56,470
So here's another way to see it.

736
00:45:56,470 --> 00:46:00,330
And this is where I'm going to define the intensity.

737
00:46:00,330 --> 00:46:03,670
So what is the intensity of an accelerator is essentially

738
00:46:03,670 --> 00:46:08,950
how much work can the accelerator do per byte transferred.

739
00:46:08,950 --> 00:46:12,230
And for any given accelerator based on the spec sheet,

740
00:46:12,230 --> 00:46:16,950
you basically have the FLOPs per second divided

741
00:46:16,950 --> 00:46:20,310
by the bytes per second.

742
00:46:20,310 --> 00:46:23,990
So how much useful work can you do per byte

743
00:46:23,990 --> 00:46:28,830
that's moved for each 100? That's 295.

744
00:46:28,830 --> 00:46:33,910
So that means for every byte of--

745
00:46:33,910 --> 00:46:38,270
you can do 295 floating point operations.

746
00:46:38,270 --> 00:46:43,390
So that's an intuitive number to have in your head, about 300.

747
00:46:43,390 --> 00:46:47,550
OK. So now the arithmetic intensity of algorithm

748
00:46:47,550 --> 00:46:51,590
is how much actual work was done per byte for this workload.

749
00:46:51,590 --> 00:46:54,670
And if you look at it for the ReLU computation,

750
00:46:54,670 --> 00:46:59,110
it's FLOPs over bytes and it's actually-- so this is actually

751
00:46:59,110 --> 00:47:02,390
a quarter, I guess, not half.

752
00:47:02,390 --> 00:47:08,170
So the point is that it's very small, 0.25. OK.

753
00:47:08,170 --> 00:47:09,910
So now we can talk about bottlenecks

754
00:47:09,910 --> 00:47:12,130
through the language of intensity.

755
00:47:12,130 --> 00:47:16,790
So something is memory bound if the arithmetic intensity is

756
00:47:16,790 --> 00:47:20,510
smaller than the accelerator intensity

757
00:47:20,510 --> 00:47:25,420
and compute bound if it's greater than accelerator intensity.

758
00:47:25,420 --> 00:47:28,140
So these are equivalent. And if you look at the algebra, it's

759
00:47:28,140 --> 00:47:29,515
basically you have two fractions.

760
00:47:29,515 --> 00:47:33,220
And then you just multiply and divide to-- you

761
00:47:33,220 --> 00:47:36,300
basically switch the terms around. OK.

762
00:47:36,300 --> 00:47:42,540
So in this case, we're memory bound.

763
00:47:42,540 --> 00:47:45,540
So in general, we're going to find ourselves in a situation

764
00:47:45,540 --> 00:47:49,180
where we're memory bound because data movement is expensive.

765
00:47:49,180 --> 00:47:52,540
And so if you can get higher arithmetic intensity,

766
00:47:52,540 --> 00:47:54,580
that's good.

767
00:47:54,580 --> 00:47:58,540
So 0.25, if you see that number, if someone tells you

768
00:47:58,540 --> 00:48:01,860
arithmetic intensity is 0.25, you should say, oh,

769
00:48:01,860 --> 00:48:03,820
this is really bad. Yeah.

770
00:48:03,820 --> 00:48:07,400
What's some typical arithmetic intensity for some transformer

771
00:48:07,400 --> 00:48:14,800
[INAUDIBLE]? Yeah, so we'll get to that. OK. All right. So OK.

772
00:48:14,800 --> 00:48:18,260
So one way to think about increasing arithmetic intensity

773
00:48:18,260 --> 00:48:22,420
is that let's just try to do more stuff per unit

774
00:48:22,420 --> 00:48:26,540
of byte moved. So the GELU is another activation.

775
00:48:26,540 --> 00:48:31,900
It looks like this, some formula that is more--

776
00:48:31,900 --> 00:48:35,900
doesn't have zeros.

777
00:48:35,900 --> 00:48:40,740
And if you do this calculation, the number of bytes are moved

778
00:48:40,740 --> 00:48:45,540
back and forth is still 2n plus 2n.

779
00:48:45,540 --> 00:48:52,540
And the FLOPs here it's about 20 FLOPs per elementwise scalar

780
00:48:52,540 --> 00:48:54,760
operation, so it's 20n.

781
00:48:54,760 --> 00:48:57,780
So the arithmetic intensity is, let's say, 5.

782
00:48:57,780 --> 00:48:59,980
This is a crude estimate.

783
00:48:59,980 --> 00:49:03,720
And in this case, are we memory bound or compute bound?

784
00:49:09,522 --> 00:49:10,980
Memory bound.

785
00:49:10,980 --> 00:49:17,060
Memory bound, because 5 is still smaller than 295, way smaller.

786
00:49:17,060 --> 00:49:24,100
So even though GELU does a lot of work, more work than ReLU,

787
00:49:24,100 --> 00:49:26,380
in the way that things are structured,

788
00:49:26,380 --> 00:49:30,020
it's still memory bound.

789
00:49:30,020 --> 00:49:33,500
Which means that if you were just computing ReLU and GELU,

790
00:49:33,500 --> 00:49:37,240
you think that, well, GELU is so complicated,

791
00:49:37,240 --> 00:49:38,640
it must be really expensive.

792
00:49:38,640 --> 00:49:40,620
But actually it's exactly the same,

793
00:49:40,620 --> 00:49:42,760
because that's not where the bottleneck is.

794
00:49:46,220 --> 00:49:49,480
OK, so now let's look at some linear operations,

795
00:49:49,480 --> 00:49:53,080
so dot product. So dot product, you have a vector x.

796
00:49:53,080 --> 00:49:56,680
You have a vector w, size n, and you take the dot product.

797
00:49:56,680 --> 00:49:58,200
So how many bytes are moved?

798
00:49:58,200 --> 00:50:01,640
So you read x, which is 2n; read w, which is 2n;

799
00:50:01,640 --> 00:50:05,760
and then you write y, which is a scalar, which is 2.

800
00:50:05,760 --> 00:50:10,620
And the number of FLOPs is you do the n multiplications

801
00:50:10,620 --> 00:50:16,900
and n minus 1 additions. So that's 2n minus 1. OK.

802
00:50:16,900 --> 00:50:19,750
So what's the arithmetic intensity?

803
00:50:19,750 --> 00:50:24,730
Oh, for this one, it's about half, which is also pretty bad.

804
00:50:24,730 --> 00:50:26,850
Which means that-- hopefully you get

805
00:50:26,850 --> 00:50:29,110
the idea-- it's memory bound.

806
00:50:33,060 --> 00:50:36,150
So what about a matrix-vector product?

807
00:50:36,150 --> 00:50:41,090
So I have this x is a vector. w is an n by n matrix.

808
00:50:41,090 --> 00:50:43,610
And you form this product.

809
00:50:43,610 --> 00:50:46,570
How many of you think this will be compute bound?

810
00:50:46,570 --> 00:50:51,290
How many of you think memory bound? OK. So let's see.

811
00:50:51,290 --> 00:50:55,050
So I'm going to read x, which is 2n; read w,

812
00:50:55,050 --> 00:50:59,170
which is 2n squared; and write y, which is 2n.

813
00:50:59,170 --> 00:51:02,090
And then the number of FLOPs is basically

814
00:51:02,090 --> 00:51:05,230
you're doing n dot products.

815
00:51:05,230 --> 00:51:09,010
So that's n times the cost of doing a dot product.

816
00:51:09,010 --> 00:51:14,030
And the arithmetic intensity is barely, barely higher.

817
00:51:16,870 --> 00:51:21,650
So this is also memory bound. OK.

818
00:51:21,650 --> 00:51:25,270
So now let's talk about matrix multiplication.

819
00:51:25,270 --> 00:51:27,690
So this is where things get interesting.

820
00:51:27,690 --> 00:51:30,230
So I have an n by m matrix, another m by n matrix.

821
00:51:30,230 --> 00:51:31,530
And I multiply them.

822
00:51:31,530 --> 00:51:38,090
And the number of bytes that were moved was 2n squared plus

823
00:51:38,090 --> 00:51:39,250
2n squared.

824
00:51:39,250 --> 00:51:43,610
And then I have to write 2n squared bytes back to y.

825
00:51:43,610 --> 00:51:49,690
And the number of FLOPs here is n squared dot products.

826
00:51:49,690 --> 00:51:53,730
And then what's arithmetic intensity? It's 300. Whew.

827
00:51:53,730 --> 00:52:01,410
OK, so 340. And in general, it's roughly n over 3.

828
00:52:01,410 --> 00:52:05,170
And intuitively this makes sense because you're

829
00:52:05,170 --> 00:52:10,350
sending n squared things, but you're computing n cubed things.

830
00:52:10,350 --> 00:52:12,610
So the number of things you're computing over

831
00:52:12,610 --> 00:52:16,240
the number of things you're sending is order n.

832
00:52:16,240 --> 00:52:19,560
And this gets better the larger you make the matrices.

833
00:52:19,560 --> 00:52:25,140
So in general, this is why when you hear people talk about, oh,

834
00:52:25,140 --> 00:52:28,780
we need to make large batch sizes or have large matrices,

835
00:52:28,780 --> 00:52:29,940
it's exactly this.

836
00:52:29,940 --> 00:52:36,360
If you're under the arithmetic intensity, making things smaller

837
00:52:36,360 --> 00:52:39,620
doesn't actually speed things up. It's all the same.

838
00:52:39,620 --> 00:52:43,460
Whereas if you get to a point where you're over this,

839
00:52:43,460 --> 00:52:48,360
then you're actually saturating your GPUs. OK.

840
00:52:48,360 --> 00:52:53,400
So finally, this is compute bound. OK.

841
00:52:53,400 --> 00:52:55,540
So as long as we have large matrices,

842
00:52:55,540 --> 00:52:56,980
we're actually pretty good.

843
00:52:56,980 --> 00:52:59,600
We're in a compute bound saturating the accelerator.

844
00:52:59,600 --> 00:53:03,300
And the question earlier about what about transformers?

845
00:53:03,300 --> 00:53:06,660
Well, it turns out we'll see in both your assignment,

846
00:53:06,660 --> 00:53:08,720
but also in the next lecture that transformers

847
00:53:08,720 --> 00:53:13,120
are essentially big matrix multiplications with some things

848
00:53:13,120 --> 00:53:14,760
sprinkled in between.

849
00:53:14,760 --> 00:53:19,372
So that's good news from arithmetic intensity.

850
00:53:19,372 --> 00:53:20,830
And this is, by design, transformer

851
00:53:20,830 --> 00:53:26,800
is designed in a certain way to have high arithmetic intensity.

852
00:53:26,800 --> 00:53:29,360
And one comment here, just to foreshadow

853
00:53:29,360 --> 00:53:32,280
the inference lecture, is that matrix vector products

854
00:53:32,280 --> 00:53:34,360
is essentially what goes on when you're

855
00:53:34,360 --> 00:53:36,200
doing transformer inference.

856
00:53:36,200 --> 00:53:39,440
Because inference, you're generating one token at a time.

857
00:53:39,440 --> 00:53:41,680
And so you only get to-- it's like a vector

858
00:53:41,680 --> 00:53:45,560
that you're trying to dot product with a matrix.

859
00:53:45,560 --> 00:53:49,960
And that's as we saw with memory bound.

860
00:53:49,960 --> 00:53:52,520
Whereas at training time, you get this whole sequence

861
00:53:52,520 --> 00:53:58,140
and you're processing all at once. OK.

862
00:53:58,140 --> 00:54:03,300
So note also that the intensity depends on the precision.

863
00:54:03,300 --> 00:54:05,920
So by default everything we're doing here is fp16.

864
00:54:08,860 --> 00:54:12,800
OK. So also to tie it to the other question about MFU,

865
00:54:12,800 --> 00:54:16,240
and the reason you might be getting low MFU is

866
00:54:16,240 --> 00:54:25,040
that while you might be doing-- so MFU is a promise FLOPs--

867
00:54:25,040 --> 00:54:28,120
sorry, actual over promise.

868
00:54:28,120 --> 00:54:32,280
So if you have really large memory bottlenecks,

869
00:54:32,280 --> 00:54:35,540
then you're not actually going to get very good throughput,

870
00:54:35,540 --> 00:54:38,800
even though you--

871
00:54:38,800 --> 00:54:41,495
the promise is if you didn't have memory bottlenecks,

872
00:54:41,495 --> 00:54:44,120
you were just like going through and doing all the computations

873
00:54:44,120 --> 00:54:45,020
of your model.

874
00:54:47,880 --> 00:54:53,000
OK. Final thing on arithmetic intensity roofline plots.

875
00:54:53,000 --> 00:54:54,880
So there's a nice way to-- yeah.

876
00:54:54,880 --> 00:55:00,000
[INAUDIBLE] generally speaking, these models

877
00:55:00,000 --> 00:55:04,440
tend to be memory bound, as they say for most of the computation.

878
00:55:04,440 --> 00:55:08,950
But yet the accelerators are 50% is not as good.

879
00:55:08,950 --> 00:55:11,790
And maybe you get 70, maybe you get 80 that's really good,

880
00:55:11,790 --> 00:55:15,430
meaning that the accelerator is outsized

881
00:55:15,430 --> 00:55:20,410
compared to memory bandwidth. Why is it like that?

882
00:55:20,410 --> 00:55:23,390
Why do these accelerators—

883
00:55:23,390 --> 00:55:27,750
are just big while they're just idling or waiting for memory?

884
00:55:27,750 --> 00:55:31,870
So the question is, could you design maybe accelerators that

885
00:55:31,870 --> 00:55:36,250
had better characteristics? Yeah.

886
00:55:36,250 --> 00:55:38,150
Maybe when we talk about GPUs and understand

887
00:55:38,150 --> 00:55:42,230
a bit more how they work, we can talk about why this is.

888
00:55:42,230 --> 00:55:44,810
But if you have an answer, you should tell Jensen.

889
00:55:44,810 --> 00:55:49,790
And maybe you can design a better hardware.

890
00:55:49,790 --> 00:55:54,390
OK, so let's visualize the relationship between arithmetic

891
00:55:54,390 --> 00:55:55,490
intensity and performance.

892
00:55:55,490 --> 00:55:59,590
So this plot basically plots the arithmetic intensity

893
00:55:59,590 --> 00:56:00,650
on the x-axis.

894
00:56:00,650 --> 00:56:05,870
So every slice here corresponds to a particular, let's say,

895
00:56:05,870 --> 00:56:09,390
algorithm. And then we have these lines here.

896
00:56:09,390 --> 00:56:12,290
And each of these lines corresponds to, let's say,

897
00:56:12,290 --> 00:56:18,990
a particular accelerator. Maybe H100 or B200 or so.

898
00:56:18,990 --> 00:56:22,670
And so what this shows is— and then on the y-axis

899
00:56:22,670 --> 00:56:26,870
is the FLOPs per second that are realized.

900
00:56:26,870 --> 00:56:32,110
So if your algorithm has low arithmetic intensity, like, ReLU

901
00:56:32,110 --> 00:56:33,710
or dot products, you're going to be

902
00:56:33,710 --> 00:56:37,630
over here, which means that the FLOPs per second realized

903
00:56:37,630 --> 00:56:42,950
is going to be not as high as your peak accelerator.

904
00:56:42,950 --> 00:56:47,070
And as you increase the memory arithmetic intensity,

905
00:56:47,070 --> 00:56:49,950
that's going to— things are going to be better.

906
00:56:49,950 --> 00:56:53,550
You're going to be able to saturate your hardware up

907
00:56:53,550 --> 00:56:55,110
until a certain point.

908
00:56:55,110 --> 00:56:57,350
And after a certain point your compute bound.

909
00:56:57,350 --> 00:57:01,530
And obviously you can't exceed the peak FLOPs.

910
00:57:04,450 --> 00:57:10,610
All right. So let's go on here. OK.

911
00:57:10,610 --> 00:57:16,550
So now I'm going to go back and talk about memory

912
00:57:16,550 --> 00:57:22,990
and compute for the operations that we need in training.

913
00:57:22,990 --> 00:57:26,990
So far we've done tensor operations, basically MatMuls.

914
00:57:26,990 --> 00:57:30,590
And we saw basically how much memory it took

915
00:57:30,590 --> 00:57:34,750
and how much compute it took and the interaction between them.

916
00:57:34,750 --> 00:57:37,850
So now let's actually think about what it takes to train.

917
00:57:37,850 --> 00:57:41,870
So here is our running example here.

918
00:57:41,870 --> 00:57:45,550
Actually it's not a linear network, just a deep network.

919
00:57:45,550 --> 00:57:49,790
So I'm going to consider a case where I have an input, which

920
00:57:49,790 --> 00:57:54,230
is a B by D input, and a number of layers

921
00:57:54,230 --> 00:57:59,790
where each layer is a D by D MatMul.

922
00:57:59,790 --> 00:58:03,900
And then that produces some set of preactivations

923
00:58:03,900 --> 00:58:07,460
and then element-wise ReLU that produces some activations

924
00:58:07,460 --> 00:58:08,600
of the first layer.

925
00:58:08,600 --> 00:58:12,020
And then this is repeated again and again.

926
00:58:12,020 --> 00:58:14,360
And the output is just going to be the same size.

927
00:58:17,260 --> 00:58:20,340
So the deep network, I mean, just

928
00:58:20,340 --> 00:58:22,660
to see what it looks like in PyTorch,

929
00:58:22,660 --> 00:58:25,620
just so it's basically a set of blocks.

930
00:58:25,620 --> 00:58:31,020
Each block has a weight vector—

931
00:58:31,020 --> 00:58:34,500
sorry, a weight matrix associated with it.

932
00:58:34,500 --> 00:58:40,500
And so when you, the number of parameters

933
00:58:40,500 --> 00:58:45,820
here is D squared times the number of layers L.

934
00:58:45,820 --> 00:58:50,220
And when you run the model on the batch of data, what we're

935
00:58:50,220 --> 00:58:54,300
doing is we're going through the layers, and each layer

936
00:58:54,300 --> 00:58:58,740
we basically apply the linear transformation and then

937
00:58:58,740 --> 00:59:02,380
a pointwise ReLU activation.

938
00:59:02,380 --> 00:59:04,080
And then we do that for all the layers.

939
00:59:04,080 --> 00:59:10,360
So this is hopefully straightforward, very simple model. OK.

940
00:59:10,360 --> 00:59:15,580
So now let's talk about gradients.

941
00:59:15,580 --> 00:59:19,260
So let's use actually an even simpler example.

942
00:59:19,260 --> 00:59:24,340
So this is just a simple linear model regression.

943
00:59:24,340 --> 00:59:33,420
So we have a vector 1, 2, 3, a weight vector 1, 1, 1.

944
00:59:33,420 --> 00:59:38,520
And we take the dot product, and we form this MSE loss. OK.

945
00:59:38,520 --> 00:59:44,300
And what happens when we take the—do the backward pass is

946
00:59:44,300 --> 00:59:48,620
that each of the variables involved in this computation

947
00:59:48,620 --> 00:59:53,120
graph has a gradient that is either set or not set.

948
00:59:53,120 --> 01:00:01,500
So the w.grad gets set to 1, 2, 3.

949
01:00:01,500 --> 01:00:04,880
So this is just basic mechanics of PyTorch.

950
01:00:04,880 --> 01:00:08,660
I think it should be familiar to all of you.

951
01:00:08,660 --> 01:00:15,480
So now the question is, how much compute does gradients take?

952
01:00:15,480 --> 01:00:21,260
OK. So let's count FLOPs for computing gradients.

953
01:00:21,260 --> 01:00:25,380
So let's take a simplified model where

954
01:00:25,380 --> 01:00:33,380
you have an x, which is BID and times w1 matrix.

955
01:00:33,380 --> 01:00:36,220
We're going to ignore this ReLU for now

956
01:00:36,220 --> 01:00:39,620
just for simplicity times w2, which is the same D by D—

957
01:00:39,620 --> 01:00:42,460
same shape D by D matrix. OK.

958
01:00:42,460 --> 01:00:47,740
I'm going to use einsum here for reasons that will become clear.

959
01:00:47,740 --> 01:00:49,980
So the first thing you do in this linear network

960
01:00:49,980 --> 01:00:55,860
is you take x and you take w1. x is batch by input dimension.

961
01:00:55,860 --> 01:01:00,090
w1 is as in by out, and you get a batch by out.

962
01:01:00,090 --> 01:01:05,372
And h2 takes h1 and w2, and it's the same story.

963
01:01:05,372 --> 01:01:06,330
These are just MatMuls.

964
01:01:09,050 --> 01:01:10,340
And then you form a loss.

965
01:01:10,340 --> 01:01:12,090
I'm just sending it to some arbitrary

966
01:01:12,090 --> 01:01:18,383
to get a number out. OK. So what happens in the backward pass,

967
01:01:18,383 --> 01:01:20,050
I'm going to call these retain gradients

968
01:01:20,050 --> 01:01:23,170
for debugging purposes, which you'll see later.

969
01:01:23,170 --> 01:01:25,650
You take the backward pass on the loss.

970
01:01:25,650 --> 01:01:27,690
And the question is, how much work

971
01:01:27,690 --> 01:01:31,610
did that-- how many floating point operations was that?

972
01:01:31,610 --> 01:01:33,230
So let's zoom in on one layer.

973
01:01:33,230 --> 01:01:39,090
Let's focus on the second layer here, so this layer.

974
01:01:39,090 --> 01:01:43,410
And so the second layer takes h1 and just multiplies it

975
01:01:43,410 --> 01:01:46,970
by a matrix w2 to get h2. And that's it.

976
01:01:46,970 --> 01:01:51,290
So if you look at the FLOPs in the forward pass,

977
01:01:51,290 --> 01:01:56,490
this is just a MatMul. And we just take the three dimensions,

978
01:01:56,490 --> 01:02:00,770
and we multiply them together and bf16. That's 2 bytes.

979
01:02:00,770 --> 01:02:02,810
Sorry, that's irrelevant.

980
01:02:02,810 --> 01:02:08,480
This is just 2 because it's an addition and a multiplication.

981
01:02:08,480 --> 01:02:12,210
So in the backward pass, what does this look like?

982
01:02:12,210 --> 01:02:15,090
So in the backward pass, if you remember your chain

983
01:02:15,090 --> 01:02:17,090
rule and your backprop algorithm,

984
01:02:17,090 --> 01:02:18,890
you have to compute two things.

985
01:02:18,890 --> 01:02:23,090
You have to compute the backward message,

986
01:02:23,090 --> 01:02:30,730
the gradient of the loss with respect to your input.

987
01:02:30,730 --> 01:02:33,250
And you also have to compute the gradient with respect

988
01:02:33,250 --> 01:02:39,370
to your parameters. And what do those gradients look like?

989
01:02:39,370 --> 01:02:43,890
This is just by the definition of these--

990
01:02:43,890 --> 01:02:46,410
I guess this is just the chain rule here written

991
01:02:46,410 --> 01:02:47,830
in einsum notation.

992
01:02:47,830 --> 01:02:54,120
You take the d loss d h2, which is h2 grad.

993
01:02:54,120 --> 01:02:59,920
That's a batch by Alt matrix, and you multiply it

994
01:02:59,920 --> 01:03:04,720
by w2, which is in by out.

995
01:03:04,720 --> 01:03:14,000
And then that gives you h1 grad, which is d loss d h1 and which

996
01:03:14,000 --> 01:03:16,160
is batch n.

997
01:03:16,160 --> 01:03:20,440
So I always get this confused whenever if you see--

998
01:03:20,440 --> 01:03:23,160
learn calculus, there's like, one of them has a transpose,

999
01:03:23,160 --> 01:03:25,700
and I forget which order to put them in.

1000
01:03:25,700 --> 01:03:27,720
And einsum I think makes it very clear,

1001
01:03:27,720 --> 01:03:32,500
because you can remember even just looking at the scalar case

1002
01:03:32,500 --> 01:03:37,160
that it has to look like h1.grad is h2.grad times w2.

1003
01:03:37,160 --> 01:03:40,800
And the only question is, how do you index things?

1004
01:03:40,800 --> 01:03:42,720
And you index things by basically

1005
01:03:42,720 --> 01:03:46,600
looking at the shape of these—and the named dimensions.

1006
01:03:46,600 --> 01:03:49,520
And in this case, it's just a matrix multiplication

1007
01:03:49,520 --> 01:03:54,400
where you are summing over the output dimension.

1008
01:03:54,400 --> 01:03:57,820
And when you do that, we can check that.

1009
01:03:57,820 --> 01:04:01,760
So h1.grad was the thing that was computed by loss.backward.

1010
01:04:01,760 --> 01:04:03,920
And this is actually indeed the same thing

1011
01:04:03,920 --> 01:04:07,120
that I wrote out here just as a sanity check.

1012
01:04:07,120 --> 01:04:08,800
So the other thing you have to compute

1013
01:04:08,800 --> 01:04:13,440
is w, the gradient with respect to the parameters.

1014
01:04:13,440 --> 01:04:18,800
And that's d loss d h2 times h1.

1015
01:04:18,800 --> 01:04:20,920
So it's the same kind of backward message that's

1016
01:04:20,920 --> 01:04:22,920
coming back but multiplying with the other thing

1017
01:04:22,920 --> 01:04:27,680
that you're not taking the gradient with respect to.

1018
01:04:27,680 --> 01:04:30,360
And you just write out the dimensions.

1019
01:04:30,360 --> 01:04:35,220
And in this case, you're summing over the batch dimension.

1020
01:04:40,324 --> 01:04:43,360
But the nice thing is that if you just look at this,

1021
01:04:43,360 --> 01:04:47,400
we know how expensive this is.

1022
01:04:47,400 --> 01:04:50,720
The number of FLOPs is essentially the product

1023
01:04:50,720 --> 01:04:52,060
of all the dimensions.

1024
01:04:52,060 --> 01:04:55,100
It doesn't matter which ones you're batching or not.

1025
01:04:55,100 --> 01:04:58,920
It's basically you enumerate over the i, j, k, and you

1026
01:04:58,920 --> 01:05:01,140
aggregate. The way that you aggregate is different,

1027
01:05:01,140 --> 01:05:05,920
but the FLOPs is the same. OK.

1028
01:05:05,920 --> 01:05:10,120
So notice that the backward pass is exactly twice as

1029
01:05:10,120 --> 01:05:12,080
expensive as the forward pass.

1030
01:05:12,080 --> 01:05:15,440
And this is because you have to take—

1031
01:05:15,440 --> 01:05:18,200
compute two gradients, one with respect

1032
01:05:18,200 --> 01:05:20,500
to the parameters for each parameter,

1033
01:05:20,500 --> 01:05:23,760
and then one with respect to the input,

1034
01:05:23,760 --> 01:05:29,980
the other thing that's not the parameter. Maybe I'll pause.

1035
01:05:29,980 --> 01:05:31,940
Any questions about that?

1036
01:05:39,580 --> 01:05:43,920
OK. So now that was just for w2.

1037
01:05:43,920 --> 01:05:46,400
And you just need to apply this to all the parameters

1038
01:05:46,400 --> 01:05:47,180
in the network.

1039
01:05:47,180 --> 01:05:52,390
And you put it all together, you will see that for this network,

1040
01:05:52,390 --> 01:05:55,350
the forward pass is 2 times the number

1041
01:05:55,350 --> 01:05:59,430
of data points, which is b times the number of parameters FLOPs.

1042
01:05:59,430 --> 01:06:01,452
And the backward pass is twice of that,

1043
01:06:01,452 --> 01:06:03,910
which is 4 times the number of data points times the number

1044
01:06:03,910 --> 01:06:05,430
of parameters FLOPs.

1045
01:06:05,430 --> 01:06:10,910
And so the grand total is 2 plus 4 is 6, and 6 times the number

1046
01:06:10,910 --> 01:06:12,730
of data points and parameters.

1047
01:06:12,730 --> 01:06:16,790
So this is where the 6 nd comes from that you might

1048
01:06:16,790 --> 01:06:18,550
have seen in various places.

1049
01:06:18,550 --> 01:06:24,310
It's just by counting forward and backward.

1050
01:06:24,310 --> 01:06:28,567
So we did this for just these deep networks.

1051
01:06:28,567 --> 01:06:30,150
But it turns out that this is actually

1052
01:06:30,150 --> 01:06:33,310
a good approximation for transformers

1053
01:06:33,310 --> 01:06:36,512
as well—as long as the context length isn't too large.

1054
01:06:36,512 --> 01:06:37,970
If the context length is too large,

1055
01:06:37,970 --> 01:06:39,637
then you get the context length squared,

1056
01:06:39,637 --> 01:06:43,950
and that's more FLOPs that isn't in this kind of accounting.

1057
01:06:47,490 --> 01:06:52,870
OK. So let's talk a bit about—so now we have gradients.

1058
01:06:52,870 --> 01:06:54,710
Now we have to—the other piece when

1059
01:06:54,710 --> 01:06:57,390
you do training is we have to do optimization.

1060
01:06:57,390 --> 01:07:01,590
So here's our deep network.

1061
01:07:01,590 --> 01:07:05,430
Just so that I'm not just giving you what's in assignment 1,

1062
01:07:05,430 --> 01:07:09,070
I'm going to use the AdaGrad optimizer, which

1063
01:07:09,070 --> 01:07:13,993
this is from—this is from 2011. This predates Adam.

1064
01:07:13,993 --> 01:07:15,910
It's where you can think about it as somewhere

1065
01:07:15,910 --> 01:07:18,590
in between SGD and Adam.

1066
01:07:18,590 --> 01:07:26,510
And basically it's SGD where you look at the second moments

1067
01:07:26,510 --> 01:07:28,350
of the gradient.

1068
01:07:28,350 --> 01:07:30,770
Momentum is where you look at the first moment gradients.

1069
01:07:30,770 --> 01:07:36,270
And Adam is where you combine the two of them.

1070
01:07:36,270 --> 01:07:39,130
I'm not going to have time to go into the optimizer details here,

1071
01:07:39,130 --> 01:07:42,590
since we're focusing mostly on the usage.

1072
01:07:42,590 --> 01:07:46,950
So we can define an optimizer.

1073
01:07:46,950 --> 01:07:54,350
And when you compute the gradients

1074
01:07:54,350 --> 01:07:57,030
and you compute the gradients and then

1075
01:07:57,030 --> 01:08:00,950
when you take an optimizer step, if you're

1076
01:08:00,950 --> 01:08:03,070
defining a new optimizer, so in assignment 1,

1077
01:08:03,070 --> 01:08:06,710
you're going to implement Adam.

1078
01:08:06,710 --> 01:08:11,810
For each of the parameter groups which are,

1079
01:08:11,810 --> 01:08:16,550
in this case w1 or w2, we look at—

1080
01:08:16,550 --> 01:08:19,029
there's something called optimizer state, which

1081
01:08:19,029 --> 01:08:23,229
是存储的-- 优化器

1082
01:08:23,229 --> 01:08:29,470
运行时使用的。我们在 AdaGrad 中计算的是

1083
01:08:29,470 --> 01:08:34,029
某种-- 是梯度平方，平方

1084
01:08:34,029 --> 01:08:36,390
梯度之和。

1085
01:08:36,390 --> 01:08:40,350
所以这里我们从优化器状态中获取它。

1086
01:08:40,350 --> 01:08:42,939
我们用当前梯度更新它，

1087
01:08:42,939 --> 01:08:47,620
然后把它存回去。然后在你更新 g2 之后，

1088
01:08:47,620 --> 01:08:50,740
再更新参数。

1089
01:08:50,740 --> 01:08:53,620
所以在 AdaGrad 中，基本上

1090
01:08:53,620 --> 01:08:57,540
除以梯度平方平均值的平方根，或者说

1091
01:08:57,540 --> 01:09:06,420
梯度平方之和。好，那我们来看。

1092
01:09:06,420 --> 01:09:13,180
所以其实我可能要跳过训练循环。

1093
01:09:13,180 --> 01:09:16,939
等等，

1094
01:09:16,939 --> 01:09:20,460
我觉得我跳过了某些本不想跳过的内容。好。

1095
01:09:20,460 --> 01:09:24,540
所以那是优化器状态，现在我们来看

1096
01:09:24,540 --> 01:09:27,720
优化器使用了多少内存。

1097
01:09:27,720 --> 01:09:29,840
或者一般来说内存使用量是多少？

1098
01:09:29,840 --> 01:09:32,939
那么对于这个网络中的参数，

1099
01:09:32,939 --> 01:09:37,020
每 L 层有 D 平方个参数。

1100
01:09:37,020 --> 01:09:41,040
如果我们用 fp16 存储，每个参数占 2 字节。

1101
01:09:41,040 --> 01:09:42,660
所以这就是参数数量。

1102
01:09:42,660 --> 01:09:50,140
激活--这是 2 乘以--

1103
01:09:50,140 --> 01:09:55,460
这是 bf16 的批次大小乘以 D 乘以层数。

1104
01:09:55,460 --> 01:09:57,580
每一层都有激活。

1105
01:09:57,580 --> 01:10:00,700
还有梯度，基本上就是

1106
01:10:00,700 --> 01:10:04,820
所有参数的一份副本。这也是 bf16。

1107
01:10:04,820 --> 01:10:08,140
而优化器状态对于每个--

1108
01:10:08,140 --> 01:10:10,660
其实这里应该是--

1109
01:10:10,660 --> 01:10:12,775
我觉得这里应该是--抱歉，我来修正一下。

1110
01:10:12,775 --> 01:10:13,400
这是个笔误。

1111
01:10:13,400 --> 01:10:15,650
这里应该是参数数量，不是参数

1112
01:10:15,650 --> 01:10:17,880
内存。这里应该是参数数量，

1113
01:10:17,880 --> 01:10:21,380
所以是参数数量乘以 2，然后这里

1114
01:10:21,380 --> 01:10:24,340
是 4 乘以参数数量。

1115
01:10:24,340 --> 01:10:25,900
原因是，

1116
01:10:25,900 --> 01:10:30,980
习惯上用 fp32 存储优化器状态以保证稳定性。

1117
01:10:30,980 --> 01:10:35,380
显然人们尝试过用 bf16。

1118
01:10:35,380 --> 01:10:38,700
结果就是，你在做平方，

1119
01:10:38,700 --> 01:10:41,740
然后对多步求平均，这并不--

1120
01:10:41,740 --> 01:10:45,020
它不太稳定。

1121
01:10:45,020 --> 01:10:48,940
而对于 AdaGrad，这里每参数占 4 个字节，

1122
01:10:48,940 --> 01:10:50,720
用于存储优化器状态。

1123
01:10:50,720 --> 01:10:54,060
Adam 会存储一阶矩和二阶

1124
01:10:54,060 --> 01:10:56,322
矩，所以是 8 个字节。

1125
01:10:56,322 --> 01:10:57,780
所以如果想想优化器

1126
01:10:57,780 --> 01:11:04,580
状态实际上占了很大一部分内存。

1127
01:11:04,580 --> 01:11:09,360
有一点需要注意，内存有两个用途。

1128
01:11:09,360 --> 01:11:12,980
一是你必须把这些东西存储在 HBM 中。

1129
01:11:12,980 --> 01:11:14,540
但另一点是它

1130
01:11:14,540 --> 01:11:22,540
必须被传输到加速器上。

1131
01:11:22,540 --> 01:11:25,420
一般来说，优化器状态

1132
01:11:25,420 --> 01:11:28,000
并不是计算瓶颈。

1133
01:11:28,000 --> 01:11:31,460
所以这里的内存大小

1134
01:11:31,460 --> 01:11:35,440
对速度性能并不是很重要，

1135
01:11:35,440 --> 01:11:42,250
但它意味着你无法在内存中容纳大模型。

1136
01:11:42,250 --> 01:11:46,990
好，那么总结一下，正如我们提到的，

1137
01:11:46,990 --> 01:11:49,290
这里的参数量是 D 乘以 D 乘以 L，

1138
01:11:49,290 --> 01:11:52,970
而 FLOPs 数量是 D 乘以 token 数

1139
01:11:52,970 --> 01:11:55,250
或数据点数乘以参数量。

1140
01:11:55,250 --> 01:11:57,090
所以对于 transformer 来说，这会

1141
01:11:57,090 --> 01:11:59,350
稍微复杂一些。

1142
01:11:59,350 --> 01:12:01,270
但你会在作业 1 中做这个。

1143
01:12:01,270 --> 01:12:04,450
你会更仔细地完成它。好。

1144
01:12:04,450 --> 01:12:05,950
我会跳过训练循环。

1145
01:12:05,950 --> 01:12:10,010
这只是一个总体回顾。

1146
01:12:10,010 --> 01:12:12,930
在结束之前，我想快速讲两点。

1147
01:12:12,930 --> 01:12:19,050
一是正如我们所见，内存确实有重要影响，

1148
01:12:19,050 --> 01:12:21,190
既影响存储大模型的能力，

1149
01:12:21,190 --> 01:12:24,050
有时也影响你的速度。

1150
01:12:24,050 --> 01:12:26,550
所以一般来说，你希望减少内存使用。

1151
01:12:26,550 --> 01:12:30,570
人们通常会做两件事。

1152
01:12:30,570 --> 01:12:34,010
一是梯度累积。

1153
01:12:34,010 --> 01:12:37,330
一般来说，你希望使用足够大的批次大小

1154
01:12:37,330 --> 01:12:40,530
以提升稳定性，

1155
01:12:40,530 --> 01:12:42,890
直到达到临界批次大小，

1156
01:12:42,890 --> 01:12:45,730
Tatsu 稍后会讲到这一点。

1157
01:12:45,730 --> 01:12:50,090
正如我们所见，激活内存随批次大小增长。

1158
01:12:50,090 --> 01:12:52,650
所以在某些时候你可能会内存不足，

1159
01:12:52,650 --> 01:12:54,550
如果你的批次大小太大。

1160
01:12:54,550 --> 01:12:58,510
所以梯度累积就是说，你计算。

1161
01:12:58,510 --> 01:13:01,170
You have these micro batches, and you compute the gradient

1162
01:13:01,170 --> 01:13:02,318
on the micro batches.

1163
01:13:02,318 --> 01:13:04,110
And then you just accumulate the gradients.

1164
01:13:04,110 --> 01:13:05,590
You don't zero out the gradients.

1165
01:13:05,590 --> 01:13:08,730
And every batch size over microbatch size

1166
01:13:08,730 --> 01:13:12,170
steps, you update the parameters and zero out the gradients.

1167
01:13:12,170 --> 01:13:16,010
OK, so this is actually a very simple code change which

1168
01:13:16,010 --> 01:13:19,530
allows you to save on compute.

1169
01:13:19,530 --> 01:13:21,330
So the other thing I'll quickly mention

1170
01:13:21,330 --> 01:13:23,250
is activation checkpointing.

1171
01:13:23,250 --> 01:13:30,480
So in training, we need to store in general the activations

1172
01:13:30,480 --> 01:13:31,230
of all the layers.

1173
01:13:31,230 --> 01:13:34,740
This is done by default. Actually, interestingly,

1174
01:13:34,740 --> 01:13:37,460
for inference we don't need to compute the gradient,

1175
01:13:37,460 --> 01:13:40,760
so we only need to store the current layer's activations.

1176
01:13:40,760 --> 01:13:50,280
But for training, the memory usage is B times D times 2 times

1177
01:13:50,280 --> 01:13:56,520
L. And the question is, can you reduce this?

1178
01:13:56,520 --> 01:13:58,440
So activation checkpointing, also known

1179
01:13:58,440 --> 01:14:01,280
as gradient checkpointing or rematerialization-- the key idea

1180
01:14:01,280 --> 01:14:04,180
here is that in the forward pass,

1181
01:14:04,180 --> 01:14:07,320
you just keep activations for only a subset of the layers.

1182
01:14:07,320 --> 01:14:10,200
And in the backward pass, you recompute the missing

1183
01:14:10,200 --> 01:14:12,658
activations from the last checkpoint.

1184
01:14:12,658 --> 01:14:14,200
That's why it's called checkpointing.

1185
01:14:14,200 --> 01:14:17,920
And this is a general trick that you see in systems

1186
01:14:17,920 --> 01:14:19,100
which is trading off.

1187
01:14:19,100 --> 01:14:23,040
If you want to reduce memory, you can just recompute things.

1188
01:14:23,040 --> 01:14:27,420
So here we're going to define this to operationalize this.

1189
01:14:27,420 --> 01:14:35,640
This is actually fairly easy. Actually, I didn't--

1190
01:14:35,640 --> 01:14:37,260
OK, maybe I didn't see the--

1191
01:14:37,260 --> 01:14:39,400
OK, so basically what you do here

1192
01:14:39,400 --> 01:14:45,320
is you have the same model, except for you just add Torch

1193
01:14:45,320 --> 01:14:47,960
utils at checkpoint on a layer.

1194
01:14:47,960 --> 01:14:50,240
So that means do this computation,

1195
01:14:50,240 --> 01:14:54,680
but don't store any of the intermediate activations.

1196
01:14:54,680 --> 01:14:58,160
Only store what is needed.

1197
01:14:58,160 --> 01:15:04,400
So this means that if you store all the activations, remember

1198
01:15:04,400 --> 01:15:06,800
in your deep network, you have the thing that's

1199
01:15:06,800 --> 01:15:10,060
pre the ReLU and the thing that's after the ReLU right.

1200
01:15:10,060 --> 01:15:12,360
So that's a lot of storage.

1201
01:15:12,360 --> 01:15:15,760
And instead if you do activation checkpointing on those blocks

1202
01:15:15,760 --> 01:15:18,180
where each block has a linear and ReLU,

1203
01:15:18,180 --> 01:15:22,240
you don't store the pre ReLU, and you save basically half.

1204
01:15:22,240 --> 01:15:24,760
You can get away with half the memory.

1205
01:15:24,760 --> 01:15:29,300
And then when you're doing the backward pass, you need g3,

1206
01:15:29,300 --> 01:15:33,320
but you can compute g3 easily from h2. OK.

1207
01:15:33,320 --> 01:15:36,200
You can go further than this.

1208
01:15:36,200 --> 01:15:39,960
You can say, well, I can store all the layers,

1209
01:15:39,960 --> 01:15:45,440
but in the extreme case, I can just store no layers.

1210
01:15:45,440 --> 01:15:49,543
And so that will be maximally memory efficient.

1211
01:15:49,543 --> 01:15:52,380
The only thing is that the compute is going to be L squared.

1212
01:15:52,380 --> 01:15:54,360
Because for every one of these layers,

1213
01:15:54,360 --> 01:15:57,080
you have to start from the beginning.

1214
01:15:57,080 --> 01:16:00,040
And maybe a sweet spot is if you store the checkpoints

1215
01:16:00,040 --> 01:16:03,920
at square root L layers, that means your activation memory is

1216
01:16:03,920 --> 01:16:08,480
square root of L, and your recomputation overhead

1217
01:16:08,480 --> 01:16:16,240
is also square root of L. So that's balanced. OK.

1218
01:16:16,240 --> 01:16:19,480
So to summarize this lecture, everything

1219
01:16:19,480 --> 01:16:21,960
is operating on tensors-- parameters, gradients,

1220
01:16:21,960 --> 01:16:25,400
activations, optimization states, data.

1221
01:16:25,400 --> 01:16:27,630
We introduced this einops, which hopefully you guys

1222
01:16:27,630 --> 01:16:30,590
can embrace as a way to think about tensor operations.

1223
01:16:30,590 --> 01:16:33,910
6 times number of data points times number of parameters

1224
01:16:33,910 --> 01:16:37,110
is a formula which now we have demystified

1225
01:16:37,110 --> 01:16:41,430
as the number of FLOPs per--for training step.

1226
01:16:41,430 --> 01:16:46,790
And actually if this is training step, this should be batch size.

1227
01:16:46,790 --> 01:16:48,870
And then we talked about arithmetic intensity

1228
01:16:48,870 --> 01:16:50,870
and roofline analysis, which allows

1229
01:16:50,870 --> 01:16:53,310
us to diagnose whether a computation is

1230
01:16:53,310 --> 01:16:56,790
memory bound or compute bound.

1231
01:16:56,790 --> 01:16:59,130
Matrix multiplications are compute bound.

1232
01:16:59,130 --> 01:17:01,870
Basically everything else is memory bound.

1233
01:17:01,870 --> 01:17:04,450
And then finally, gradient accumulation, activation,

1234
01:17:04,450 --> 01:17:07,470
checkpointing are ways to reduce the memory.

1235
01:17:07,470 --> 01:17:10,190
And by reducing the memory, that allows

1236
01:17:10,190 --> 01:17:14,110
you to use bigger batch sizes. OK.

1237
01:17:14,110 --> 01:17:16,530
So that's it for today's lecture.

1238
01:17:16,530 --> 01:17:20,780
So next week, Tatsu will talk about architectures.
